window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2023-06-1", "source": "2023年06月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay that begins with the sentence \"Today there is a growing awareness that mental well-being needs to be given as much attention as physical health.\" You can make comments, cite examples or use your personal experiences to develop your essay. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words", "sample": "Today there is a growing awareness that mental well-being needs to be given as much attention as physical\nhealth. For too long, we have measured a person's health only by the body, ignoring the mind that quietly\nsuffers behind a healthy-looking face. Yet a troubled mind can be just as disabling as a broken leg, which is\nwhy the two deserve equal care.\nMy own experience convinces me of this. During my sophomore year, a close friend kept a perfect exercise\nroutine but slid into deep anxiety before exams. He looked strong, so no one noticed until his grades and sleep\ncollapsed. Only after he sought counseling did he truly recover. His story shows that neglecting the mind can\nundo all the benefits of a fit body.\nTherefore, we should treat mental and physical health as two wheels of the same cart. Schools and workplaces\nought to offer counseling as readily as gyms, and each of us should learn to talk about our feelings without\nshame. When the mind is cared for as much as the body, a person can be genuinely, fully healthy."}, "translation": {"prompt": "近年来，越来越多的中国文化产品走向全球市场， 日益受到海外消费者的青睐。随着中国对外文化贸易的快速发展，中国文化产品出口额已持续多年位居世界前列，形成了一批具有国际影响力的文化企业、产品和品牌。数据显示，中国的出版物、影视作品、网络文学与动漫作品等在海外的销售量连年攀升。中国政府出台了一系列政策鼓励和支持更多具有中国元素的优秀文化产品走出国门，扩大海外市场份额，进一步提升中国文化的世界影响力。", "sample": "In recent years, more and more Chinese cultural products have been entering the global market and winning increasing favor among overseas consumers. With the rapid development of China's foreign cultural trade, the export value of Chinese cultural products has ranked among the world's top for many years, forming a number of internationally influential cultural enterprises, products and brands. Data show that the overseas sales of China's publications, film and TV works, online literature and animation works have been rising year by year. The Chinese government has introduced a series of policies to encourage and support more fine cultural products with Chinese elements to go global, expand their overseas market share, and further enhance the global influence of Chinese culture."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2023-06-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Scientists recently examined studies on dog intelligence and compared them with research into the minds of other intelligent animals. The researchers found that dogs are among the more intelligent carnivores(食肉动物), social hunters and domestic animals, but that their intelligence does not  __26__  other intelligent animals in any of those categories. Though a significant body of research has examined dog cognition  __27__  , the authors of this new study found little to warrant the  __28__  of work that has been devoted to the topic.\nStephen Lea, lead author of the new study, argues that many researchers seem to have designed their studies to  __29__  how clever dogs are, rather than simply to study dogs' brains. Lea and a colleague examined more than 300 studies of dog cognition, comparing the studies' results with those from research into other animals. The researchers made specific comparisons between the different species in different categories of intelligence. These comparisons  __30__  that dogs are intelligent, but their intelligence is not as  __31__  as some researchers might have believed.\nIn many areas, though, comparisons were not possible. For example, the researchers noted that both dogs and cats are known to be able to recognize and  __32__  human voices. But the investigators could not find any data to indicate which species can remember a greater number of  __33__  human voices, so it was impossible to compare the two on that front. However, not all researchers agree  __34__  with the findings of this study. Zachary Silver, an American researcher, believes the authors of the new study  __35__  the idea that an excessive amount of research has been devoted to dogs, as the field of dog cognition is young, and there is much to be learned about how dogs think.", "matchIntro": "The lifesaving power of gratitude", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "Gratitude may be more beneficial than we commonly suppose. One recent study asked participants to write a note of thanks to someone and then estimate how surprised and happy the recipient would feel—an impact that they consistently underestimated. Another study assessed the health benefits of writing thank-you notes. The researchers found that writing as few as three weekly thank-you notes over the course of three weeks improved life satisfaction, increased happy feelings and reduced symptoms of depression."}, {"label": "B", "text": "While this research into gratitude is relatively new, the principles involved are anything but. Students of mine in a political philosophy course at Indiana University are reading Daniel Defoe's 300-year-old Robinson Crusoe, often regarded as the first novel published in English. Left alone on an unknown island with no apparent prospect of rescue or escape, Crusoe has much to lament(悲叹). But instead of giving in to despair, he makes a list of things for which he is grateful, including the fact that he is the sole survivor from the shipwreck(海难) and has been able to salvage many useful items from the wreckage."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Defoe's masterpiece, which is often ranked as one of the world's greatest novels, provides a portrait of gratitude in action that is as timely and relevant today as it has ever been. It is also one with which contemporary psychology and medicine are just beginning to catch up. Simply put, for most of us, it is far more helpful to focus on the things in life for which we can express gratitude than those that incline us toward resentment and lamentation."}, {"label": "D", "text": "When we focus on the things we regret, such as failed relationships, family disputes, and setbacks in career and finance, we tend to become more regretful. Conversely, when we focus on the things we are grateful for, a greater sense of happiness tends to spread through our lives. And while no one would argue for cultivating a false sense of blessedness, there is mounting evidence that counting our blessings is one of the best habits we can develop to promote mental and physical health."}, {"label": "E", "text": "Gratitude has long enjoyed a privileged position in many of the world's cultural traditions. For example, some ancient Western philosophers counsel gratitude that is both enduring and complete, and some Eastern thinkers portray it as not merely an attitude but a virtue to be put into practice."}, {"label": "F", "text": "Recent scientific studies support these ancient teachings. Individuals who regularly engage in gratitude exercises, such as counting their blessings or expressing gratitude to others, exhibit increased satisfaction with relationships and fewer symptoms of physical illness. And the benefits are not only psychological and physical. They may also be moral—those who practice gratitude also view their lives less materialistically and suffer from less envy."}, {"label": "G", "text": "There are multiple explanations for such benefits of gratefulness. One is the fact that expressing gratitude encourages others to continue being generous, thus promoting a virtuous cycle of goodness in relationships. Similarly, grateful people may be more likely to reciprocate(回报) with acts of kindness of their own. Broadly speaking, a community in which people feel grateful to one another is likely to be a more pleasant place to live than one characterized by mutual suspicion and resentment. The beneficial effects of gratitude may extend even further. For example, when many people feel good about what someone else has done for them, they experience a sense of being lifted up, with a corresponding enhancement of their regard for humanity. Some are inspired to attempt to become better people themselves, doing more to help bring out the best in others and bringing more goodness into the world around them."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Gratitude also tends to strengthen a sense of connection with others. When people want to do good things that inspire gratitude, the level of dedication in relationships tends to grow and relationships seem to last longer. And when people feel more connected, they are more likely to choose to spend their time with one another and demonstrate their feelings of affection in daily acts."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Of course, acts of kindness can also foster discomfort. For example, if people feel they are not worthy of kindness or suspect that some ulterior(别有用心的) motive lies behind it, the benefits of gratitude will not be realized. Likewise, receiving a kindness can give rise to a sense of indebtedness, leaving beneficiaries feeling that they must now pay back whatever good they have received. Gratitude can flourish only if people are secure enough in themselves and sufficiently trusting to allow it to do so. Another obstacle to gratitude is often called a sense of entitlement. Instead of experiencing a benefaction(善行) as a good turn, people sometimes regard it as a mere payment of what they are owed, for which no one deserves any moral credit."}, {"label": "J", "text": "There are a number of practical steps anyone can take to promote a sense of gratitude. One is simply spending time on a regular basis thinking about someone who has made a difference, or perhaps writing a thank-you note or expressing such gratitude in person. Others are found in ancient religious disciplines, such as reflecting on benefactions received from another person or actually praying for the health and happiness of a benefactor. In addition to benefactions received, it is also possible to focus on opportunities to do good oneself, whether those acted on in the past or hoped for in the future. Some people are most grateful not for what others have done for them but for chances they enjoyed to help others. In regularly reflecting on the things in his life he is grateful for, Defoe's Crusoe believes that he becomes a far better person than he would have been had he remained in the society from which he originally set out on his voyage."}, {"label": "K", "text": "Reflecting on generosity and gratitude, the great basketball coach John Wooden once offered two counsels to his players and students. First, he said, \"It is impossible to have a perfect day unless you have done something for someone who will never be able to repay you.\" In saying this, Wooden sought to promote purely generous acts, as opposed to those performed with an expectation of reward. Second, he said, \"Give thanks for your blessings every day.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Some faith traditions incorporate such practices into the rhythm of daily life. For example, adherents of some religions offer prayers of thanksgiving every morning before rising and every night before lying down to sleep. Others offer thanks throughout the day, such as before meals. Other less frequent special events, such as births, deaths and marriages, may also be heralded by such prayers."}, {"label": "M", "text": "When Defoe depicted Robinson Crusoe making thanksgiving a daily part of his island life, he was anticipating findings in social science and medicine that would not appear for hundreds of years. Yet he was also reflecting the wisdom of religious and philosophical traditions that extend back thousands of years. Gratitude is one of the healthiest and most nourishing of all states of mind, and those who adopt it as a habit are enriching not only their own lives but also the lives of those around them."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Technology is never a neutral tool for achieving human ends. Technological innovations reshape people as they use these innovations to control their environment. Artificial intelligence, for example, is altering humanity.\nWhile the term AI conjures up anxieties about killer robots or catastrophic levels of unemployment, there are other, deeper implications. As AI increasingly shapes the human experience, how does this change what it means to be human? Central to the problem is a person's capacity to make choices, particularly judgments that have moral implications.\nAristotle argued that the capacity for making practical judgments depends on regularly making them—on habit and practice. We see the emergence of machines as substitute judges in a variety of everyday contexts as a potential threat to people learning how to effectively exercise judgment themselves.\nIn the workplace, managers routinely make decisions about who to hire or fire and which loan to approve, to name a few. These are areas where algorithmic(算法的) prescription is replacing human judgment, and so people who might have had the chance to develop practical judgment in these areas no longer will.\nRecommendation engines, which are increasingly prevalent intermediaries in people's consumption of culture, may serve to constrain choice and minimize luck. By presenting consumers with algorithmically selected choices of what to watch, read, stream and visit next, companies are replacing human taste with machine taste. In one sense, this is helpful. After all, machines can survey a wider range of choices than any individual is likely to have the time or energy to do on their own.\nAt the same time, though, this selection is optimizing for what people are likely to prefer based on what they've preferred in the past. We think there is some risk that people's options will be constrained by their past in a new and unanticipated way.\nAs machine learning algorithms improve and as they train on more extensive data sets, larger parts of everyday life are likely to become utterly predictable. The predictions are going to get better and better, and they will ultimately make common experiences more efficient and pleasant.\nAlgorithms could soon—if they don't already—have a better idea about which show you'd like to watch next and which job candidate you should hire than you do. One day, humans may even find a way for machines to make these decisions without some of the biases that humans typically display.\nBut to the extent that unpredictability is part of how people understand themselves and part of what people like about themselves, humanity is in the process of losing something significant. As they become more and more predictable, the creatures inhabiting the increasingly AI-mediated world will become less and less like us.", "47": "Technology is never a neutral tool for achieving human ends. Technological innovations reshape people as they use these innovations to control their environment. Artificial intelligence, for example, is altering humanity.\nWhile the term AI conjures up anxieties about killer robots or catastrophic levels of unemployment, there are other, deeper implications. As AI increasingly shapes the human experience, how does this change what it means to be human? Central to the problem is a person's capacity to make choices, particularly judgments that have moral implications.\nAristotle argued that the capacity for making practical judgments depends on regularly making them—on habit and practice. We see the emergence of machines as substitute judges in a variety of everyday contexts as a potential threat to people learning how to effectively exercise judgment themselves.\nIn the workplace, managers routinely make decisions about who to hire or fire and which loan to approve, to name a few. These are areas where algorithmic(算法的) prescription is replacing human judgment, and so people who might have had the chance to develop practical judgment in these areas no longer will.\nRecommendation engines, which are increasingly prevalent intermediaries in people's consumption of culture, may serve to constrain choice and minimize luck. By presenting consumers with algorithmically selected choices of what to watch, read, stream and visit next, companies are replacing human taste with machine taste. In one sense, this is helpful. After all, machines can survey a wider range of choices than any individual is likely to have the time or energy to do on their own.\nAt the same time, though, this selection is optimizing for what people are likely to prefer based on what they've preferred in the past. We think there is some risk that people's options will be constrained by their past in a new and unanticipated way.\nAs machine learning algorithms improve and as they train on more extensive data sets, larger parts of everyday life are likely to become utterly predictable. The predictions are going to get better and better, and they will ultimately make common experiences more efficient and pleasant.\nAlgorithms could soon—if they don't already—have a better idea about which show you'd like to watch next and which job candidate you should hire than you do. One day, humans may even find a way for machines to make these decisions without some of the biases that humans typically display.\nBut to the extent that unpredictability is part of how people understand themselves and part of what people like about themselves, humanity is in the process of losing something significant. As they become more and more predictable, the creatures inhabiting the increasingly AI-mediated world will become less and less like us.", "48": "Technology is never a neutral tool for achieving human ends. Technological innovations reshape people as they use these innovations to control their environment. Artificial intelligence, for example, is altering humanity.\nWhile the term AI conjures up anxieties about killer robots or catastrophic levels of unemployment, there are other, deeper implications. As AI increasingly shapes the human experience, how does this change what it means to be human? Central to the problem is a person's capacity to make choices, particularly judgments that have moral implications.\nAristotle argued that the capacity for making practical judgments depends on regularly making them—on habit and practice. We see the emergence of machines as substitute judges in a variety of everyday contexts as a potential threat to people learning how to effectively exercise judgment themselves.\nIn the workplace, managers routinely make decisions about who to hire or fire and which loan to approve, to name a few. These are areas where algorithmic(算法的) prescription is replacing human judgment, and so people who might have had the chance to develop practical judgment in these areas no longer will.\nRecommendation engines, which are increasingly prevalent intermediaries in people's consumption of culture, may serve to constrain choice and minimize luck. By presenting consumers with algorithmically selected choices of what to watch, read, stream and visit next, companies are replacing human taste with machine taste. In one sense, this is helpful. After all, machines can survey a wider range of choices than any individual is likely to have the time or energy to do on their own.\nAt the same time, though, this selection is optimizing for what people are likely to prefer based on what they've preferred in the past. We think there is some risk that people's options will be constrained by their past in a new and unanticipated way.\nAs machine learning algorithms improve and as they train on more extensive data sets, larger parts of everyday life are likely to become utterly predictable. The predictions are going to get better and better, and they will ultimately make common experiences more efficient and pleasant.\nAlgorithms could soon—if they don't already—have a better idea about which show you'd like to watch next and which job candidate you should hire than you do. One day, humans may even find a way for machines to make these decisions without some of the biases that humans typically display.\nBut to the extent that unpredictability is part of how people understand themselves and part of what people like about themselves, humanity is in the process of losing something significant. As they become more and more predictable, the creatures inhabiting the increasingly AI-mediated world will become less and less like us.", "49": "Technology is never a neutral tool for achieving human ends. Technological innovations reshape people as they use these innovations to control their environment. Artificial intelligence, for example, is altering humanity.\nWhile the term AI conjures up anxieties about killer robots or catastrophic levels of unemployment, there are other, deeper implications. As AI increasingly shapes the human experience, how does this change what it means to be human? Central to the problem is a person's capacity to make choices, particularly judgments that have moral implications.\nAristotle argued that the capacity for making practical judgments depends on regularly making them—on habit and practice. We see the emergence of machines as substitute judges in a variety of everyday contexts as a potential threat to people learning how to effectively exercise judgment themselves.\nIn the workplace, managers routinely make decisions about who to hire or fire and which loan to approve, to name a few. These are areas where algorithmic(算法的) prescription is replacing human judgment, and so people who might have had the chance to develop practical judgment in these areas no longer will.\nRecommendation engines, which are increasingly prevalent intermediaries in people's consumption of culture, may serve to constrain choice and minimize luck. By presenting consumers with algorithmically selected choices of what to watch, read, stream and visit next, companies are replacing human taste with machine taste. In one sense, this is helpful. After all, machines can survey a wider range of choices than any individual is likely to have the time or energy to do on their own.\nAt the same time, though, this selection is optimizing for what people are likely to prefer based on what they've preferred in the past. We think there is some risk that people's options will be constrained by their past in a new and unanticipated way.\nAs machine learning algorithms improve and as they train on more extensive data sets, larger parts of everyday life are likely to become utterly predictable. The predictions are going to get better and better, and they will ultimately make common experiences more efficient and pleasant.\nAlgorithms could soon—if they don't already—have a better idea about which show you'd like to watch next and which job candidate you should hire than you do. One day, humans may even find a way for machines to make these decisions without some of the biases that humans typically display.\nBut to the extent that unpredictability is part of how people understand themselves and part of what people like about themselves, humanity is in the process of losing something significant. As they become more and more predictable, the creatures inhabiting the increasingly AI-mediated world will become less and less like us.", "50": "Technology is never a neutral tool for achieving human ends. Technological innovations reshape people as they use these innovations to control their environment. Artificial intelligence, for example, is altering humanity.\nWhile the term AI conjures up anxieties about killer robots or catastrophic levels of unemployment, there are other, deeper implications. As AI increasingly shapes the human experience, how does this change what it means to be human? Central to the problem is a person's capacity to make choices, particularly judgments that have moral implications.\nAristotle argued that the capacity for making practical judgments depends on regularly making them—on habit and practice. We see the emergence of machines as substitute judges in a variety of everyday contexts as a potential threat to people learning how to effectively exercise judgment themselves.\nIn the workplace, managers routinely make decisions about who to hire or fire and which loan to approve, to name a few. These are areas where algorithmic(算法的) prescription is replacing human judgment, and so people who might have had the chance to develop practical judgment in these areas no longer will.\nRecommendation engines, which are increasingly prevalent intermediaries in people's consumption of culture, may serve to constrain choice and minimize luck. By presenting consumers with algorithmically selected choices of what to watch, read, stream and visit next, companies are replacing human taste with machine taste. In one sense, this is helpful. After all, machines can survey a wider range of choices than any individual is likely to have the time or energy to do on their own.\nAt the same time, though, this selection is optimizing for what people are likely to prefer based on what they've preferred in the past. We think there is some risk that people's options will be constrained by their past in a new and unanticipated way.\nAs machine learning algorithms improve and as they train on more extensive data sets, larger parts of everyday life are likely to become utterly predictable. The predictions are going to get better and better, and they will ultimately make common experiences more efficient and pleasant.\nAlgorithms could soon—if they don't already—have a better idea about which show you'd like to watch next and which job candidate you should hire than you do. One day, humans may even find a way for machines to make these decisions without some of the biases that humans typically display.\nBut to the extent that unpredictability is part of how people understand themselves and part of what people like about themselves, humanity is in the process of losing something significant. As they become more and more predictable, the creatures inhabiting the increasingly AI-mediated world will become less and less like us.", "51": "Phonics, which involves sounding out words syllable(音节) by syllable, is the best way to teach children to read. But in many classrooms, this can be a dirty word. So much so that some teachers have had to sneak phonics teaching materials into the classroom. Most American children are taught to read in a way that study after study has found to be wrong.\nThe consequences of this are striking. Less than half of all American adults were proficient readers in 2017. American fourth graders rank 15th on the Progress in International Literacy Study, an international exam.\nAmerica is stuck in a debate about teaching children to read that has been going on for decades. Some advocate teaching symbol-sound relationships (the sound k can be spelled as c, k, ck, or ch), known as phonics. Others support an immersive approach (using pictures of a cat to learn the word cat), known as \"whole language\". Most teachers today, almost three out of four according to a survey by the Ed Week Research Centre in 2019, use a mix called \"balanced literacy\". This combination of methods is ineffective. \"You can't sprinkle in a little phonics,\" says Tenette Smith, executive director of elementary education and reading at Mississippi's education department. \"It has to be systematic and explicitly taught.\"\nMississippi, often behind in social policy, has set an example here. In a state once notorious for its low reading scores, the Mississippi state legislature passed new literacy standards in 2013. Since then Mississippi has seen remarkable gains. Its fourth graders have moved from 49th (out of 50 states) to 29th on the National Assessment of Educational Progress, a nationwide exam. In 2019 it was the only state to improve its scores. For the first time since measurement began, Mississippi's pupils are now average readers, a remarkable achievement in such a poor state.\nMississippi's success is attributed to implementing reading methods supported by a body of research known as the science of reading. In 1997 Congress requested the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and the Department of Education to convene a National Reading Panel to end the \"reading wars\" and synthesize the evidence. The panel found that phonics, along with explicit instruction in phonemic(音位的) awareness, fluency and comprehension, worked best.\nYet over two decades on, \"balanced literacy\" is still being taught in classrooms. But advances in statistics and brain imaging have disproved the whole-language method. To the teacher who is a proficient reader, literacy seems like a natural process that requires educated guessing, rather than the deliberate process emphasized by phonics. Teachers can imagine that they learned to read through osmosis(潜移默化) when they were children. Without proper training, they bring this to classrooms.", "52": "Phonics, which involves sounding out words syllable(音节) by syllable, is the best way to teach children to read. But in many classrooms, this can be a dirty word. So much so that some teachers have had to sneak phonics teaching materials into the classroom. Most American children are taught to read in a way that study after study has found to be wrong.\nThe consequences of this are striking. Less than half of all American adults were proficient readers in 2017. American fourth graders rank 15th on the Progress in International Literacy Study, an international exam.\nAmerica is stuck in a debate about teaching children to read that has been going on for decades. Some advocate teaching symbol-sound relationships (the sound k can be spelled as c, k, ck, or ch), known as phonics. Others support an immersive approach (using pictures of a cat to learn the word cat), known as \"whole language\". Most teachers today, almost three out of four according to a survey by the Ed Week Research Centre in 2019, use a mix called \"balanced literacy\". This combination of methods is ineffective. \"You can't sprinkle in a little phonics,\" says Tenette Smith, executive director of elementary education and reading at Mississippi's education department. \"It has to be systematic and explicitly taught.\"\nMississippi, often behind in social policy, has set an example here. In a state once notorious for its low reading scores, the Mississippi state legislature passed new literacy standards in 2013. Since then Mississippi has seen remarkable gains. Its fourth graders have moved from 49th (out of 50 states) to 29th on the National Assessment of Educational Progress, a nationwide exam. In 2019 it was the only state to improve its scores. For the first time since measurement began, Mississippi's pupils are now average readers, a remarkable achievement in such a poor state.\nMississippi's success is attributed to implementing reading methods supported by a body of research known as the science of reading. In 1997 Congress requested the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and the Department of Education to convene a National Reading Panel to end the \"reading wars\" and synthesize the evidence. The panel found that phonics, along with explicit instruction in phonemic(音位的) awareness, fluency and comprehension, worked best.\nYet over two decades on, \"balanced literacy\" is still being taught in classrooms. But advances in statistics and brain imaging have disproved the whole-language method. To the teacher who is a proficient reader, literacy seems like a natural process that requires educated guessing, rather than the deliberate process emphasized by phonics. Teachers can imagine that they learned to read through osmosis(潜移默化) when they were children. Without proper training, they bring this to classrooms.", "53": "Phonics, which involves sounding out words syllable(音节) by syllable, is the best way to teach children to read. But in many classrooms, this can be a dirty word. So much so that some teachers have had to sneak phonics teaching materials into the classroom. Most American children are taught to read in a way that study after study has found to be wrong.\nThe consequences of this are striking. Less than half of all American adults were proficient readers in 2017. American fourth graders rank 15th on the Progress in International Literacy Study, an international exam.\nAmerica is stuck in a debate about teaching children to read that has been going on for decades. Some advocate teaching symbol-sound relationships (the sound k can be spelled as c, k, ck, or ch), known as phonics. Others support an immersive approach (using pictures of a cat to learn the word cat), known as \"whole language\". Most teachers today, almost three out of four according to a survey by the Ed Week Research Centre in 2019, use a mix called \"balanced literacy\". This combination of methods is ineffective. \"You can't sprinkle in a little phonics,\" says Tenette Smith, executive director of elementary education and reading at Mississippi's education department. \"It has to be systematic and explicitly taught.\"\nMississippi, often behind in social policy, has set an example here. In a state once notorious for its low reading scores, the Mississippi state legislature passed new literacy standards in 2013. Since then Mississippi has seen remarkable gains. Its fourth graders have moved from 49th (out of 50 states) to 29th on the National Assessment of Educational Progress, a nationwide exam. In 2019 it was the only state to improve its scores. For the first time since measurement began, Mississippi's pupils are now average readers, a remarkable achievement in such a poor state.\nMississippi's success is attributed to implementing reading methods supported by a body of research known as the science of reading. In 1997 Congress requested the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and the Department of Education to convene a National Reading Panel to end the \"reading wars\" and synthesize the evidence. The panel found that phonics, along with explicit instruction in phonemic(音位的) awareness, fluency and comprehension, worked best.\nYet over two decades on, \"balanced literacy\" is still being taught in classrooms. But advances in statistics and brain imaging have disproved the whole-language method. To the teacher who is a proficient reader, literacy seems like a natural process that requires educated guessing, rather than the deliberate process emphasized by phonics. Teachers can imagine that they learned to read through osmosis(潜移默化) when they were children. Without proper training, they bring this to classrooms.", "54": "Phonics, which involves sounding out words syllable(音节) by syllable, is the best way to teach children to read. But in many classrooms, this can be a dirty word. So much so that some teachers have had to sneak phonics teaching materials into the classroom. Most American children are taught to read in a way that study after study has found to be wrong.\nThe consequences of this are striking. Less than half of all American adults were proficient readers in 2017. American fourth graders rank 15th on the Progress in International Literacy Study, an international exam.\nAmerica is stuck in a debate about teaching children to read that has been going on for decades. Some advocate teaching symbol-sound relationships (the sound k can be spelled as c, k, ck, or ch), known as phonics. Others support an immersive approach (using pictures of a cat to learn the word cat), known as \"whole language\". Most teachers today, almost three out of four according to a survey by the Ed Week Research Centre in 2019, use a mix called \"balanced literacy\". This combination of methods is ineffective. \"You can't sprinkle in a little phonics,\" says Tenette Smith, executive director of elementary education and reading at Mississippi's education department. \"It has to be systematic and explicitly taught.\"\nMississippi, often behind in social policy, has set an example here. In a state once notorious for its low reading scores, the Mississippi state legislature passed new literacy standards in 2013. Since then Mississippi has seen remarkable gains. Its fourth graders have moved from 49th (out of 50 states) to 29th on the National Assessment of Educational Progress, a nationwide exam. In 2019 it was the only state to improve its scores. For the first time since measurement began, Mississippi's pupils are now average readers, a remarkable achievement in such a poor state.\nMississippi's success is attributed to implementing reading methods supported by a body of research known as the science of reading. In 1997 Congress requested the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and the Department of Education to convene a National Reading Panel to end the \"reading wars\" and synthesize the evidence. The panel found that phonics, along with explicit instruction in phonemic(音位的) awareness, fluency and comprehension, worked best.\nYet over two decades on, \"balanced literacy\" is still being taught in classrooms. But advances in statistics and brain imaging have disproved the whole-language method. To the teacher who is a proficient reader, literacy seems like a natural process that requires educated guessing, rather than the deliberate process emphasized by phonics. Teachers can imagine that they learned to read through osmosis(潜移默化) when they were children. Without proper training, they bring this to classrooms.", "55": "Phonics, which involves sounding out words syllable(音节) by syllable, is the best way to teach children to read. But in many classrooms, this can be a dirty word. So much so that some teachers have had to sneak phonics teaching materials into the classroom. Most American children are taught to read in a way that study after study has found to be wrong.\nThe consequences of this are striking. Less than half of all American adults were proficient readers in 2017. American fourth graders rank 15th on the Progress in International Literacy Study, an international exam.\nAmerica is stuck in a debate about teaching children to read that has been going on for decades. Some advocate teaching symbol-sound relationships (the sound k can be spelled as c, k, ck, or ch), known as phonics. Others support an immersive approach (using pictures of a cat to learn the word cat), known as \"whole language\". Most teachers today, almost three out of four according to a survey by the Ed Week Research Centre in 2019, use a mix called \"balanced literacy\". This combination of methods is ineffective. \"You can't sprinkle in a little phonics,\" says Tenette Smith, executive director of elementary education and reading at Mississippi's education department. \"It has to be systematic and explicitly taught.\"\nMississippi, often behind in social policy, has set an example here. In a state once notorious for its low reading scores, the Mississippi state legislature passed new literacy standards in 2013. Since then Mississippi has seen remarkable gains. Its fourth graders have moved from 49th (out of 50 states) to 29th on the National Assessment of Educational Progress, a nationwide exam. In 2019 it was the only state to improve its scores. For the first time since measurement began, Mississippi's pupils are now average readers, a remarkable achievement in such a poor state.\nMississippi's success is attributed to implementing reading methods supported by a body of research known as the science of reading. In 1997 Congress requested the National Institute of Child Health and Human Development and the Department of Education to convene a National Reading Panel to end the \"reading wars\" and synthesize the evidence. The panel found that phonics, along with explicit instruction in phonemic(音位的) awareness, fluency and comprehension, worked best.\nYet over two decades on, \"balanced literacy\" is still being taught in classrooms. But advances in statistics and brain imaging have disproved the whole-language method. To the teacher who is a proficient reader, literacy seems like a natural process that requires educated guessing, rather than the deliberate process emphasized by phonics. Teachers can imagine that they learned to read through osmosis(潜移默化) when they were children. Without proper training, they bring this to classrooms."}, "questions": [{"id": "2023-06-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What was the woman's first impression of the apartment?", "options": ["A) It was spacious and tranquil.", "B) It was warm and comfortable.", "C) It was shabby and solitary.", "D) It was tiny and noisy."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It was warm and comfortable.\n【定位】Well, last week when I moved in, the apartment seemed cozy, just the right size for one\nperson. But now it just seems tiny, shabby and solitary.\n嗯，上周我刚搬进来时，公寓显得很温馨，对一个人来说大小正合适。但现在它只让人觉得\n又小、又破、又孤单。\n【信号】时间对照 last week…But now 切出'第一印象'。答案句靠 last week when I moved in 与\nBut now 形成前后对照：转折前讲的是刚搬进来时（即第一印象），转折后才是现在的感\n受。题问 first impression，要取转折前那半句 the apartment seemed cozy, just the\nright size，别被 But now 后面的负面词带走。\n【替换】the apartment seemed cozy, just the right size for one person ↔ It was warm and comfortable.\ncozy=warm and comfortable 的同义概括\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nIt was spacious and tranquil. 说'宽敞安静'，但录音只说 just the right size for one\nperson（一人正合适），并非宽敞，tranquil 安静也无对应，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：just the right size for one person\nC) 原词陷阱\nshabby and solitary 两词录音确实念到，但出现在 But now it just seems tiny, shabby\nand solitary——是'现在'的感受，不是题问的'第一印象'，盗用原词答非所问。\n借用原文：But now it just seems tiny, shabby and solitary\nD) 原词陷阱\nIt was tiny and noisy. 中 tiny 是转折后'现在'的词（now it just seems tiny），noisy 录音\n根本没说；既错时间点又有未提及成分。\n借用原文：now it just seems tiny", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "Why does the woman say she is going to sound like an idiot?", "options": ["A) She no longer hates people talking loudly in the dorm.", "B) She misses her roommates she used to complain about.", "C) She begins to enjoy the movies she once found irritating.", "D) She finds the crowded dorm as cozy as her new apartment."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） She misses her roommates she used to complain about.\n【定位】I'm going to sound like an idiot, because I used to complain to you all the time about how\ncrowded our dormitory room was, and about all the things they did to irritate me… But now I\nmiss them terribly.\n我接下来说的会显得很傻，因为我以前老是跟你抱怨宿舍多么拥挤、室友们做的种种让我心\n烦的事……可现在我却特别想念他们。\n【信号】because 引出原因 + But now 给出反转落点。答案句由 because 直接引出'为何像傻瓜'的\n原因，又用 But now I miss them terribly 把态度反转到落点：以前抱怨、现在却想念，这\n一反差正是她说自己像傻瓜的缘由。because 与 But now 两个信号叠加锁定此句。\n【替换】I used to complain to you all the time about how crowded our dormitory room was ↔\nshe used to complain about\nused to complain 原义保留、对象概括为 roommates\nBut now I miss them terribly ↔ She misses her roommates\nmiss them=misses her roommates，代词还原\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nShe no longer hates people talking loudly in the dorm. 盗用录音 talking loudly early\nin the morning 这个原词，但录音说的是'这曾让我心烦'，并没说'不再讨厌'，no longer\n是凭空加的极性反转。\n借用原文：talking loudly early in the morning\nC) 原词陷阱\nShe begins to enjoy the movies she once found irritating. 把录音 watching movies\nlate at night without headphones 和 irritate 拼起来，但录音只说这些事曾让她心烦，从\n未说她现在'开始享受电影'，属答非所问。\n借用原文：watching movies late at night without headphones\nD) 原词陷阱\nShe finds the crowded dorm as cozy as her new apartment. 借用 crowded、cozy 等\n词，但录音只说想念室友，从未把'拥挤的宿舍'与'新公寓'做'一样温馨'的比较，属无中生\n有。\n借用原文：how crowded our dormitory room was", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What do we learn about the man when he left the dormitory to live on his own?", "options": ["A) He found the apartment perfectly furnished.", "B) He had a feeling of despair and frustration.", "C) He had a similar feeling to the woman's.", "D) He felt the new place was like paradise."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He had a similar feeling to the woman's.\n【定位】When I got my first place I remember thinking I couldn't wait to live by myself… But then I\nbegan missing them and feeling lonely and thinking that our dormitory was like paradise,\neven though there were six of us guys in one small room.\n我有了自己第一个住处时，记得当时心想终于可以一个人住了……可后来我开始想念他们、\n感到孤单，觉得我们的宿舍简直像天堂，哪怕当时我们六个大男生挤在一间小屋里。\n【信号】But then 后的'想念、孤单'与女方感受平行。男方先说当初迫不及待独居，But then I\nbegan missing them and feeling lonely 转折后才是真实感受：想念室友、感到孤单。这\n与女方'now I miss them terribly'完全平行，正应了选项'与女方感受相似'。But then 是锁\n定真实感受的信号。\n【替换】But then I began missing them and feeling lonely ↔ He had a similar feeling to the woman's.\n男方'想念+孤单'平行于女方同款感受=similar feeling\n【排除】A) 未提及\nHe found the apartment perfectly furnished. 录音里男方谈的是情绪（想念、孤单），\n从未说他的住处家具完美齐备，属未提及。\nB) 过度推断\nHe had a feeling of despair and frustration. 把男方的 feeling lonely（孤单）夸大成\ndespair（绝望）、frustration（沮丧），情绪程度被大幅拔高，属过度推断。\n借用原文：feeling lonely\nD) 张冠李戴\nHe felt the new place was like paradise. 盗用原词 paradise，但录音 thinking that our\ndormitory was like paradise 说的是'宿舍像天堂'，绝非'新住处像天堂'，把对象张冠李戴\n了。\n借用原文：thinking that our dormitory was like paradise", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What does the man say he can't wait to do?", "options": ["A) Go to see the woman's apartment.", "B) Make a phone call to his parents.", "C) Buy some furniture for the woman.", "D) Decorate the woman's apartment."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Go to see the woman's apartment.\n【定位】That does sound adorable and I can't wait to see it, and neither can mom and dad.\n那听起来确实很可爱，我都等不及要去看看了，爸妈也一样等不及呢。\n【信号】意愿标志 I can't wait to see it 直接给出动作。答案句的信号就是 I can't wait to see it，\ncan't wait to 后面的 see it 即男方迫不及待要做的事；it 指上文女方的公寓。题干问 can't\nwait to do，顺着这个词在原文里一找即得。\n【替换】I can't wait to see it ↔ Go to see the woman's apartment.\nsee it→go to see the apartment，代词还原\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nMake a phone call to his parents. 借用录音 neither can mom and dad 里的父母，但男\n方说的是'爸妈也等不及（想看公寓）'，并非'要打电话给父母'，属答非所问。\n借用原文：neither can mom and dad\nC) 未提及\nBuy some furniture for the woman. 中 furniture 与女方描述公寓时提到的 sofa、table\n沾边，但男方从未说要买家具，属未提及。\nD) 张冠李戴\nDecorate the woman's apartment. 盗用原词 decorate，但录音 now that I have\ndecorated it 说的是女方已经装饰好了，并非男方迫不及待要去装饰，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：now that I have decorated it", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn from the conversation about the man?", "options": ["A) He works as a literary critic.", "B) He hosts an educational program.", "C) He has initiated a university reform.", "D) He has published a book recently."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） He has published a book recently.\n【定位】Our guest today is Frank Jones, a critic of our education system, and the author of a new\nbook, How to Reform Our Universities.\n我们今天的嘉宾是 Frank Jones，他是我国教育体制的一位批评者，也是一本新书《如何改\n革我们的大学》的作者。\n【信号】开场介绍嘉宾句。答案信号是主持人开篇介绍嘉宾的固定句式 Our guest today is Frank\nJones，这类“今天的嘉宾是……，他是……的作者”的引荐句，必然交代人物身份，是细节题\n的天然落点。\n【替换】the author of a new book, How to Reform Our Universities ↔ He has published a book recently.\n“新书作者”=“最近出书”\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确说他是 a critic of our education system（教育体制的批评者），A 盗这个 critic 改\n成 a literary critic.（文学评论家）——把“批评教育”偷换成“文学评论”，是听到 critic 就想\n选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：a critic of our education system\nB) 张冠李戴\nHe hosts an educational program.（他主持一档教育节目）说反了人物：主持节目的是女\n方 Susan（Welcome to our program Book Talk），男方只是受访嘉宾，把主持人的身份\n安到嘉宾头上。\n借用原文：Welcome to our program Book Talk\nC) 过度推断\nHe has initiated a university reform.（他已发起一场大学改革）盗用了书名里的 reform\n（How to Reform Our Universities）。他只是写书“主张”改革，并未真的发起改革，把“写\n书谈改革”夸成“已发起改革”，属过度推断。\n借用原文：How to Reform Our Universities", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man believe is the problem with the current American system of higher education?", "options": ["A) It fails to keep up with the radical changes of society.", "B) It fails to ensure universities get sufficient resources.", "C) It has not prepared young people for the job market.", "D) It has not fostered the growth of the arts disciplines."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It has not prepared young people for the job market.\n【定位】I believe that the purpose of higher education is to prepare young people to enter the\nworkforce, and that our current system fails to do this.\n我认为高等教育的目的是让年轻人为进入职场做好准备，而我们现行的体制没能做到这\n一点。\n【信号】被问者应答 + fails 否定。答案信号有二：一是这是男方对“你支持激进改革大学吗”的直接\n应答（被问者的回答处），二是句中 our current system fails to do this 的否定动词\nfails，正点出“制度没做到的事”——即他眼中的问题。\n【替换】prepare young people to enter the workforce ↔\nIt has not prepared young people for the job market.\nworkforce=job market\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nIt fails to keep up with the radical changes of society. 借用了原文 radically changing\n（radically changing universities）的 radical，但男方批的是“没对接就业”，并未说制度\n“跟不上社会的剧变”，把原词拼到一个原文没说的归因上。\n借用原文：radically changing universities\nB) 与原文相反\nIt fails to ensure universities get sufficient resources. 盗用原文 resources（allocating\ntoo many resources）。男方说的是资源“分配错地方（过多给了不匹配需求的学科）”，不\n是大学“拿不到足够资源”，方向正相反。\n借用原文：allocating too many resources to disciplines that don't match the needs of employers\nD) 与原文相反\nIt has not fostered the growth of the arts disciplines.（没扶持文科）与男方立场完全相\n反：他主张少投文科、多投理工（take money from subjects like literature and\nmusic），怎会嫌文科扶持不够。这是把他的对立面安到他头上。\n借用原文：take money from subjects like literature and music", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "How should the education budget be allocated according to the man?", "options": ["A) More of the budget should go to science and technology.", "B) The underfunded music discipline should be prioritized.", "C) Subdisciplines like sculpture should get more funding.", "D) Literature should get as much funding as engineering."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） More of the budget should go to science and technology.\n【定位】My argument is that we need to use more of our budget on areas like science and engineering.\nTo do that, we need to take money from subjects like literature and music.\n我的主张是，我们需要把更多预算用在科学和工程这类领域。要做到这一点，我们就得从文\n学、音乐这类科目那里抽走经费。\n【信号】立论句 My argument is。答案信号是男方亮明主张的标志句 My argument is that we\nneed to use more of our budget on areas like science and engineering——My\nargument is / we need to 是典型的“建议 / 主张”信号，其后内容即他的真实立场。\n【替换】use more of our budget on areas like science and engineering ↔\nMore of the budget should go to science and technology.\n“多用预算于”=“更多预算应给”\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\nThe underfunded music discipline should be prioritized. 盗用原文 music（literature\nand music）。男方恰恰主张从 music 抽钱（take money from），而非优先扶持\nmusic，方向完全相反。\n借用原文：take money from subjects like literature and music\nC) 张冠李戴\nSubdisciplines like sculpture should get more funding. 借用女方后文提到的 sculpture\n（literature or music or sculpture）。但这是女方为艺术辩护时举的例，男方的主张是给\n理工加钱、从文科抽钱，绝不会主张给雕塑这类细分文科加经费，既张冠李戴又方向相反。\n借用原文：Studying literature or music or sculpture\nD) 偷换概念\nLiterature should get as much funding as engineering. 盗用 literature、engineering\n两个原词，却把男方“偏向理工、从文学抽钱”的不均等主张，偷换成“文学与工程经费同\n等”。男方要的是倾斜，不是均分。\n借用原文：take money from subjects like literature and music", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman say the arts can do?", "options": ["A) Build a prosperous nation.", "B) Make skilled professionals.", "C) Create ingenious artists.", "D) Cultivate better citizens."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Cultivate better citizens.\n【定位】Studying literature or music or sculpture might not result in a job in that area, but it helps\nyoung people to think about the world in a deeper way, which makes them better citizens and\nmakes for a better society.\n学习文学、音乐或雕塑，也许不会带来该领域的工作，但它能帮助年轻人更深入地思考世\n界，从而让他们成为更好的公民、造就更好的社会。\n【信号】转折 but 后才是真意。答案信号是转折词 but——but it helps young people to think\nabout the world in a deeper way, which makes them better citizens。but 之前的\nmight not result in a job 是让步铺垫，but 之后才是女方真正要强调的艺术功效。\n【替换】which makes them better citizens ↔ Cultivate better citizens.\n“使其成为更好公民”=“培养更好公民”\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\nBuild a prosperous nation. 听上去接近原文 makes for a better society，但 prosperous\nnation（繁荣国家）与 better society（更好的社会）并不等同，且题问“艺术能做什么”的\n核心落点是 better citizens；把“更好的社会”拔成“建设繁荣国家”是偷换加拔高。\n借用原文：makes for a better society\nB) 未提及\nMake skilled professionals.（培养技术能手）原文从未出现。女方讲的是艺术让人“更深地\n思考、成为更好公民”，与“培养职业技能人才”无关，属凭空添加。\nC) 原词陷阱\nCreate ingenious artists. 紧贴 arts 话题，最像“学艺术就是培养艺术家”。但女方明说学艺\n术 might not result in a job in that area（未必能从事该行），其价值在育人而非造艺术\n家，恰与“培养艺术家”相左。\n借用原文：might not result in a job in that area", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we learn about the feeling that one doesn't have time to think anymore?", "options": ["A) It is quite common.", "B) It is rarely noticed.", "C) It seldom annoys people.", "D) It occurs when one is alone."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is quite common.\n【定位】Do you ever have the annoying feeling that you don't have time to really think anymore? You\nare not alone.\n你是否曾有那种烦人的感觉——觉得自己再也没时间好好思考了？你并不是一个人（很多人\n都这样）。\n【信号】应答式呼应 You are not alone。录音先抛出 \"没时间思考\" 的感受，紧接一句 You are not\nalone.——\"你不是一个人\" 是对前面感受的直接回应与定性，等于说 \"这事很多人都有\"，\n这种自问自答后的定性句就是答案信号。\n【替换】You are not alone. ↔ It is quite common.\n换说法表 \"普遍\"\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n录音说这是 the annoying feeling（烦人的感觉），明明是 \"会让人烦\"，选项 It is rarely\nnoticed. 却说 \"很少被注意到\"，与录音强调它令人困扰相反——听到 annoying 就该排除\n\"没人注意\"。\n借用原文：the annoying feeling\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 It seldom annoys people. 抠了录音原词 annoying 来骗 \"听到就想选\"，却把意思反\n过来说成 \"很少惹人烦\"；录音的 the annoying feeling 恰恰说它烦人，故 C 与原文相反。\n借用原文：the annoying feeling\nD) 张冠李戴\n选项 It occurs when one is alone. 的 \"独处时才发生\" 在第 1 段并无依据；alone 一词是\n录音末段 When you're alone, stop worrying and think. 才出现、且讲的是 \"独处时该怎\n么做\"，与第 9 题这种感受何时发生无关，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：When you're alone, stop worrying and think.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What trait do all great professionals share?", "options": ["A) Seeing things in black and white.", "B) Engaging in regular contemplation.", "C) Having a special understanding of creativity.", "D) Knowing how to make their mental batteries work."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Engaging in regular contemplation.\n【定位】Regular reflection, however, underlies all great professionals.\n然而，定期的反思是所有伟大专业人士的根基（共同特质）。\n【信号】转折 however + 全称 all。句首 however 的转折把语气拉回正题，紧跟 underlies all\ngreat professionals 用全称 all 概括所有专业人士的共同点——转折后 + 全称主语，正是\n答案句的双重信号。\n【替换】Regular reflection ↔ Engaging in regular contemplation.\nreflection=contemplation 同义替换\nunderlies all great professionals ↔ Engaging in regular contemplation.\n\"是根基\" ↔ \"从事此事\"\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音说反思能让人 see things in a new light（以新眼光看事物），选项 Seeing things in\nblack and white. 抠 \"seeing things\" 半句，却篡改成 \"非黑即白地看\"，与 \"用新视角\" 含\n义相反，是偷换。\n借用原文：see things in a new light\nC) 过度推断\n录音只说反思能 tap into your creativity（激发你的创造力），并没说专业人士对创造力有\n\"特殊理解\"；选项 Having a special understanding of creativity. 借原词 creativity，把\n\"激发创造力\" 过度引申成 \"特殊理解\"，无中生有。\n借用原文：tap into your creativity\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说反思是 \"recharge your mental batteries（给心理电池充电）\" 的前提，选项\nKnowing how to make their mental batteries work. 抠原词 mental batteries，却把意\n思偷换成 \"懂得怎么让电池运转\"——录音讲的是 \"靠反思来充电\"，不是 \"懂得让电池工作\"\n这种能力，答非所问。\n借用原文：recharge your mental batteries", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What do some researchers believe is conducive to creative ideas?", "options": ["A) Engaging in intense activity.", "B) Fantasizing in one's downtime.", "C) Working on a particular project.", "D) Reflecting during one's relaxation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Reflecting during one's relaxation.\n【定位】Some researchers in the field of creativity, in fact, believe that insight occurs during the\nreflection and relaxation that follows a period of intense activity.\n事实上，创造力领域的一些研究者认为，灵感出现在一段高强度活动之后的反思与放松\n期间。\n【信号】观点归属 believe that 引出落点。句子用 Some researchers…believe that 把观点归到研\n究者名下，正对题干所问；其后 insight occurs during the reflection and relaxation 才\n是真正回答 \"什么利于创意\" 的落点，须听到 believe that 后那整段才算抓全。\n【替换】insight occurs during the reflection and relaxation ↔ Reflecting during one's relaxation.\n灵感发生于反思放松时 ↔ 放松时反思\ninsight ↔ conducive to creative ideas\ninsight=creative ideas\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说灵感发生在 \"高强度活动之后\" 的反思放松期，选项 Engaging in intense activity.\n却把原词 intense activity 拎出来，说成 \"灵感来自从事高强度活动本身\"——录音明明讲的\n是 \"活动之后\" 而非 \"活动之中\"，时点被偷换。\n借用原文：a period of intense activity\nB) 张冠李戴\n选项 Fantasizing in one's downtime. 的 fantasizing、downtime 来自末段 fantasizing\nabout how we'd like our lives to be，而那里恰恰说幻想是浪费 down time、要被纠正\n的，并非利于创意；既张冠李戴又取反义。\n借用原文：fantasizing about how we'd like our lives to be\nC) 答非所问\n选项 Working on a particular project. 的 particular project 来自第 3 段 a particular\nproject you're engaged in，那是讲 \"为某个项目安排反思时间\" 的操作建议，并非研究者\n所说的 \"创意来源\"；本题问的是 conducive to creative ideas，与做项目无关。\n借用原文：a particular project you're engaged in", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the passage say about Colorado before it became a state?", "options": ["A) Farmers helped Native Americans grow crops.", "B) There were expansive university campuses.", "C) There existed post offices.", "D) Migrants found gold there."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） There existed post offices.\n【定位】Even before Colorado was a state, it had post offices. The first opened in 1859, in a\nsettlement founded by migrants searching for gold.\n甚至在科罗拉多成为一个州之前，它就已经有了邮局。第一家邮局于 1859 年在一个由寻金\n移民建立的定居点开张。\n【信号】开篇 before…was a state 直接点题。录音开篇即用 Even before Colorado was a state,\nit had post offices，before…a state 与题干完全对应，是直接给出事实的点题句，无需转\n折即可锁定。\n【替换】it had post offices ↔ There existed post offices.\nhad 与 There existed 同义换说\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nFarmers helped Native Americans grow crops. 借用了录音里的 Native Americans，但\n原文是 settlers clashed with Native Americans，讲的是定居者与原住民发生冲突，根本\n没有农民帮原住民种地这回事。\n借用原文：settlers clashed with Native Americans\nB) 原词陷阱\nThere were expansive university campuses. 借了录音的 university campus，但原文是\nOn the settlement's location now stands a sprawling university campus，明说大学校\n园是如今才矗立在那里的，不是建州前就有，时间被偷换。\n借用原文：now stands a sprawling university campus\nD) 与原文相反\nMigrants found gold there. 借了录音的 gold 和 migrants，但原文是 gold failed to\nappear，明说金子并未出现，移民是去找金而没找到，与选项说的找到金正好相反。\n借用原文：gold failed to appear", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "How did the postal service contribute to America's westward expansion?", "options": ["A) It helped to boost the economy in the American West.", "B) It provided job opportunities for many gold seekers.", "C) It extended the influence of the federal government.", "D) It kept people in the deserts and plains connected."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It kept people in the deserts and plains connected.\n【定位】As federal subsidies and land grants tempted people into the deserts and plains, the post kept\nthem connected.\n当联邦补贴和土地授予吸引人们进入沙漠和平原时，邮政把他们连接在了一起。\n【信号】the post kept them connected 直陈作用。the post kept them connected 直接陈述邮\n政起的作用是把进入沙漠平原的人连接起来，是本段讲贡献的落点句，主语 the post 与题\n干主体对应，无需转折即可锁定。\n【替换】the post kept them connected ↔ It kept people in the deserts and plains connected.\nthem 还原为 the deserts and plains 里的 people\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt helped to boost the economy in the American West. 听上去合理，但录音从未说邮政\n提振了西部经济，本段只讲邮政把人连接起来，提振经济属于录音没说的内容。\nB) 未提及\nIt provided job opportunities for many gold seekers. 蹭了前文寻金移民的话题，但录音\n讲邮政的作用是连接，从未说它给寻金者提供了就业机会，是把没说的就业作用安到邮政头\n上。\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt extended the influence of the federal government. 借了录音的 federal，原文确有\nbig government 和 federal subsidies，但那是说西部开拓靠大政府，并非说邮政扩大了政\n府影响力，邮政被原文定的作用是连接而非扩张政府影响。\n借用原文：Colonization of the West could be regarded as a result of big government", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What did the federal government do to meet the increasing demand for the postal service in the West?", "options": ["A) It employed Native Americans to work as postmen.", "B) It commissioned private wagons to carry the mail.", "C) It subsidized the locals who acted as postmasters.", "D) It centralized postal services in its remote areas."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It commissioned private wagons to carry the mail.\n【定位】The federal government commissioned private wagons to carry the mail.\n联邦政府委托私人马车来运送邮件。\n【信号】主语 federal government 加动作 commissioned 直陈做法。The federal government\ncommissioned private wagons to carry the mail 以题干主体 federal government 作主\n语、紧跟动作 commissioned，直接陈述政府的做法，是讲政府措施那段的落点句。\n【替换】commissioned private wagons to carry the mail ↔\nIt commissioned private wagons to carry the mail.\n几乎原句搬用，仅主语换为 It\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\nIt employed Native Americans to work as postmen. 借了全篇出现的 Native\nAmericans，但录音从未说政府雇原住民当邮差，本段讲的是委托私人马车和聘当地商人，\n把没说的对象安进了动作里。\n借用原文：settlers clashed with Native Americans\nC) 原词陷阱\nIt subsidized the locals who acted as postmasters. 借了录音的 postmasters 和\nlocals，但原文是 Short-term contracts were granted to local businessmen to act as\npostmasters，讲的是把短期合同授予当地商人，是 granted contracts 而非 subsidized，\n动作被偷换。\n借用原文：Short-term contracts were granted to local businessmen to act as postmasters\nD) 与原文相反\nIt centralized postal services in its remote areas. 与原文相反，原文明说 the Post\nOffice Department was far from a centralized bureaucracy，即邮政部门远非集中化机\n构，选项却说政府把偏远地区邮政集中化，极性相反。\n借用原文：the Post Office Department was far from a centralized bureaucracy", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What did Mr. Blevins do to study the history of the US postal service?", "options": ["A) He analyzed interactive maps of mail routes.", "B) He read a large collection of books on the topic.", "C) He examined its historical trends with data science.", "D) He collected data about its impact on local business."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） He examined its historical trends with data science.\n【定位】He used data science to analyze historical trends.\n他运用数据科学来分析历史趋势。\n【信号】used…to 方法句直陈研究手段。He used data science to analyze historical trends 用\nused…to 句式直接陈述他用数据科学分析历史趋势，是讲研究方法的核心句，主语 He 与题\n干 Mr. Blevins 对应。\n【替换】used data science to analyze historical trends ↔\nHe examined its historical trends with data science.\nused…to analyze 与 examined…with 同义换说\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nHe analyzed interactive maps of mail routes. 借了录音的 interactive maps，但原文是\nhe built an accompanying website, complete with interactive maps，地图是他做出来\n的成果，不是他用来研究的方法，方向被偷换。\n借用原文：he built an accompanying website, complete with interactive maps\nB) 与原文相反\nHe read a large collection of books on the topic. 借了录音的 book，但原文是 wrote a\nbook on the history of the US postal service，他是写了一本书，不是读了大量书，动作\n正好相反。\n借用原文：wrote a book on the history of the US postal service\nD) 原词陷阱\nHe collected data about its impact on local business. 借了 data 一词，但录音只说他用\n数据科学分析趋势，从未说他收集关于对本地商业影响的数据，impact on local business\n是录音没有的内容。\n借用原文：He used data science to analyze historical trends", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say the new study suggests?", "options": ["A) Higher levels of anxiety may improve people's memory.", "B) Some experiences are easier to remember than others.", "C) Most people tend to remember things selectively.", "D) Simple things may leave a deep impression on one's memory."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Higher levels of anxiety may improve people's memory.\n【定位】In fact, a new study suggests that people with higher anxiety levels might remember certain\ninformation better than people with lower anxiety levels.\n事实上，一项新研究表明，焦虑水平较高的人，可能比焦虑水平较低的人更能记住某些\n信息。\n【信号】提示词 a new study suggests 后即结论。录音里 a new study suggests that 是明确的\n结论引出语，凡听到\"研究表明\"这类话，紧跟其后的整句就是答案句，本句把 higher\nanxiety 与 remember…better 直接挂钩，正是研究结论。\n【替换】people with higher anxiety levels might remember certain information better ↔\nHigher levels of anxiety may improve people's memory\n\"记得更好\"改写成\"提升记忆\"\npeople with higher anxiety levels ↔ Higher levels of anxiety\n名词化平移\n【排除】D) 原词陷阱\nSimple things may leave a deep impression 盗用了录音 make a deep impression on\nyour memory 里的 deep impression，但原文说的是\"买咖啡这种简单事本来不会留下深\n刻印象\"，是用来引出焦虑可改变这一点的铺垫反例，并非研究结论，听到 deep\nimpression 就选会中招。\n借用原文：make a deep impression on your memory\nC) 未提及\nMost people tend to remember things selectively 说\"多数人有选择地记忆\"，但录音只\n说高焦虑者\"记得更好\"(remember…better)，从没说\"有选择地\"(selectively)，也没说 most\npeople，范围和限定词都是凭空加的。\nB) 答非所问\nSome experiences are easier to remember than others（有些经历比另一些更易记）听\n上去对，但它只说\"经历有难易之分\"，完全没提研究的关键——是 anxiety 在起作用，把研\n究最核心的变量丢了，等于答非所问。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What did researchers do first in the new study?", "options": ["A) They classified the participants' mindset.", "B) They showed some photos to the participants.", "C) They measured the participants' anxiety levels.", "D) They tested the size of the participants' vocabulary."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） They measured the participants' anxiety levels.\n【定位】In the study, the researchers started by giving 80 undergraduate students an anxiety test. The\ntest measured the participants' anxiety levels over the preceding two weeks.\n在这项研究中，研究者首先给 80 名本科生做了一个焦虑测试。该测试测量了被试在此前两\n周里的焦虑水平。\n【信号】顺序信号 started by 标出第一步。录音 the researchers started by giving 80\nundergraduate students an anxiety test 用 started by（一开始就…）明确标出研究的第\n一步，这是顺序题的定位灵魂，听到 started by 就锁定其后那个动作为\"第一步\"。\n【替换】giving 80 undergraduate students an anxiety test ↔ measured the participants' anxiety levels\n做焦虑测试=测焦虑水平\n80 undergraduate students ↔ the participants\n上位词替换\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nshowed some photos to the participants 盗用了录音 Some of the words were printed\non to photos 里的 photos，看照片确实是研究的一步，但它发生在测焦虑之后（原文\nThen…the participants were shown…），把它当成 first 就掉进了顺序陷阱。\n借用原文：Some of the words were printed on to photos of negative scenes\nD) 原词陷阱\ntested the size of the participants' vocabulary 借了录音反复出现的 words（词），让人\n误以为在测词汇，但原文用词只是给被试看 a series of neutral words 作记忆材料，从没测\n过\"词汇量大小\"，testing vocabulary 纯属无中生有。\n借用原文：the participants were shown a series of neutral words\nA) 张冠李戴\nclassified the participants' mindset 说给被试的心态分类，录音里确有 negative/neutral\nmindset（消极/中性心态）的说法，但那是描述被试回想词语后进入的状态，研究者并没\n有\"对被试心态做分类\"这一操作步骤，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：caused them to reenter either a negative or a neutral mindset", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What do we learn from previous studies about anxiety?", "options": ["A) Anxiety has become a serious problem for an increasing number of people.", "B) Extreme levels of anxiety can adversely affect cognitive performance.", "C) People diagnosed with anxiety disorder may forget things selectively.", "D) There is no direct correlation between memory and levels of anxiety."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Extreme levels of anxiety can adversely affect cognitive performance.\n【定位】Previous studies have found that extreme levels of anxiety, such as those experienced\nby people diagnosed with an anxiety disorder, can be quite detrimental to memory and\ncognitive performance.\n以往的研究发现，极端水平的焦虑——比如被诊断患有焦虑症的人所经历的那种——可能对\n记忆和认知表现相当有害。\n【信号】来源词 Previous studies 引出答案句。录音 Previous studies have found that 直接对应\n题干的 previous studies，凡题干限定的来源词在原文原样出现，那一句就是答案句，本句\n给出极端焦虑\"有害于记忆和认知\"的发现。\n【替换】extreme levels of anxiety ↔ Extreme levels of anxiety\n程度词原样保留\ncan be quite detrimental to memory and cognitive performance ↔\ncan adversely affect cognitive performance\ndetrimental=adversely affect\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nPeople diagnosed with anxiety disorder may forget things selectively 盗用了录音\npeople diagnosed with an anxiety disorder 这个原词组，但原文说焦虑症患者的极端焦\n虑\"有害于记忆和认知\"，从没说他们\"有选择地遗忘(forget…selectively)\"，selectively 是凭\n空加的。\n借用原文：those experienced by people diagnosed with an anxiety disorder\nD) 与原文相反\nThere is no direct correlation between memory and levels of anxiety 说焦虑与记忆\"无\n直接关联\"，可录音 previous studies 明说极端焦虑 detrimental to memory，本研究又说\n高焦虑者\"记得更好\"，处处都在讲二者有关联，D 把结论说反了。\n借用原文：can be quite detrimental to memory and cognitive performance\nA) 与原文相反\nAnxiety has become a serious problem for an increasing number of people 说焦虑成\n了越来越多人的严重问题，但原文末句恰恰说本研究里的高焦虑者 anxiety is not a serious\nproblem（焦虑对他们不是严重问题），且全文谈的是\"以往研究\"而非\"人数增多\"，A 既偏\n题又与原文相反。\n借用原文：anxiety is not a serious problem", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about today's consumers?", "options": ["A) They compare products from different companies before making a choice.", "B) They get information from other consumers' postings and comments.", "C) They lose patience when their phone call is not promptly answered.", "D) They expect to get instantaneous responses to their inquiry."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They expect to get instantaneous responses to their inquiry.\n【定位】Today, they want the information immediately. They'll go to the company's social media page\nand post comments and questions, expecting to receive an immediate response.\n今天，他们想要立刻得到信息。他们会跑到公司的社交媒体页面发评论、提问题，期待立刻\n收到回应。\n【信号】对比信号 Today 后的真意。前文用 in the past 讲过去打电话耐心等待，Today, they want\nthe information immediately 用 Today 一转，今昔对比里 Today 之后才是讲\"今天的消费\n者\"，正是题干所问，这就是答案句。\n【替换】expecting to receive an immediate response ↔\nexpect to get instantaneous responses to their inquiry\nimmediate=instantaneous 同义替换\npost comments and questions ↔ to their inquiry\n提问=inquiry 概括替换\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nlose patience when their phone call is not promptly answered 借了录音 call a phone\nnumber and patiently wait on hold 的\"打电话/等待\"原词，但那是 in the past 描述过去\n的做法，且录音说的是\"耐心等待\"，并非\"失去耐心\"，时间和意思双错。\n借用原文：call a phone number and patiently wait on hold\nB) 原词陷阱\nget information from other consumers' postings and comments 借了录音 post\ncomments and questions 的\"发帖/评论\"原词，但录音说的是消费者自己去公司页面发问\n以求快速回应，并没有说他们\"从其他消费者\"那里获取信息，多塞了 other consumers 这\n一层。\n借用原文：post comments and questions\nA) 未提及\ncompare products from different companies before making a choice 说的是\"比较不\n同公司的产品再选\"，录音通篇没讲消费者比价，只讲他们求快、求即时回应，属凭常识臆\n造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "How do marketers capture consumers' attention as quickly as possible?", "options": ["A) Giving them rewards on the spot.", "B) Broadening their scope of interest.", "C) Speaking directly to their emotions.", "D) Focusing on the details of the product."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Speaking directly to their emotions.\n【定位】Marketers need to figure out ways to speak directly to the customers' emotions, and they need\nto figure out how to do that as quickly as possible.\n营销者需要想办法直接打动顾客的情感，而且要想办法尽可能快地做到这一点。\n【信号】建议信号 need to + 题干同词。Marketers need to figure out ways to 用 need to 给出\n明确做法，而且原句紧跟 as quickly as possible，和题干 as quickly as possible 逐字呼\n应，正是答案句。\n【替换】speak directly to the customers' emotions ↔ Speaking directly to their emotions\n几乎原句重述\n【排除】D) 未提及\nFocusing on the details of the product 听起来像营销常识，但录音讲的是打动情感、\n是\"人们想看视频而非读文字\"，反而暗示不要堆细节文字，原文并未让营销者聚焦产品细\n节。\nB) 未提及\nBroadening their scope of interest 录音从未提及\"拓宽消费者兴趣范围\"，全篇讲的是注\n意力变短、要快速打动情感，方向不搭。\nA) 未提及\nGiving them rewards on the spot 的\"当场给奖励\"录音通篇未提，与\"打动情感、抓住短暂\n注意力\"的主线无关。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the speaker suggest marketers do to meet future challenges?", "options": ["A) Change the rules of the game in the market every year.", "B) Keep up with the latest technological developments.", "C) Learn from technological innovators to do business.", "D) Make greater efforts to build up consumers' confidence."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Keep up with the latest technological developments.\n【定位】That's why it's important for marketers to pay attention to the latest technological\ndevelopments and consider collaborating with technological innovators.\n这就是为什么营销者关注最新的技术发展、并考虑与技术创新者合作很重要。\n【信号】结论信号 That's why + it's important。末段先讲未来挑战(信心被动摇、规则年年变)，\n再用 That's why it's important for marketers to 引出建议，That's why 加 it's\nimportant 是双重结论信号，这句即答案句。\n【替换】pay attention to the latest technological developments ↔\nKeep up with the latest technological developments\npay attention to=keep up with 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nChange the rules of the game in the market every year 借了录音 the rules of the\ngame change every year 的原话，但录音说的是\"规则每年在变\"(这是营销者面临的客观挑\n战/原因)，绝不是建议营销者\"去改变规则\"，把客观处境偷换成了主观建议。\n借用原文：the rules of the game change every year\nC) 原词陷阱\nLearn from technological innovators to do business 借了录音 collaborating with\ntechnological innovators 的\"科技创新者\"原词，但录音说的是\"与之合作\"(collaborating\nwith)，不是\"向其学习做生意\"(learn from...to do business)，动作被偷换。\n借用原文：collaborating with technological innovators\nD) 原词陷阱\nMake greater efforts to build up consumers' confidence 借了录音 Their confidence is\nbeing shaken 的 confidence 原词，但原文说的是\"营销者自己的信心被动摇\"，根本不\n是\"消费者的信心\"，且讲者没建议去\"建立消费者信心\"，对象和建议双错。\n借用原文：Their confidence is being shaken", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say is interesting?", "options": ["A) People have only one social engagement per week.", "B) Working together enhances friendship.", "C) Few people have devoted friends.", "D) Friendships benefit work."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Friendships benefit work.\n【定位】You might be surprised to learn that the benefits of friendships extend beyond people's social\nlife and into their work, which is interesting when considering the extent to which people\nsacrifice friendships … because of the extended hours they're devoting to work.\n你或许会惊讶地发现，友谊的好处不止于社交生活，还延伸进了工作之中；考虑到人们为了\n延长工作时间而牺牲友谊，这一点尤其有意思。\n【信号】interesting 一词所在句，即题干所指那句。录音里 which is interesting 的 interesting 与\n题干 interesting 完全对应，它前面那句 the benefits of friendships extend beyond\npeople's social life and into their work 就是讲者说『有意思』的内容，这是直接的词面\n信号，听到 interesting 即锁定。\n【替换】the benefits of friendships extend beyond people's social life and into their work ↔\nFriendships benefit work.\n『友谊好处延伸进工作』概括为『友谊有益工作』\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA:People have only one social engagement per week. 里的 one social engagement\nper week 是讲者后面 I'm content with only one social engagement per week 自述的\n个人情况，被搬来当选项，但题干问的是『什么有意思』，这只是讲者举的自身例子，答非\n所问。\n借用原文：I'm content with only one social engagement per week\nB) 与原文相反\nB:Working together enhances friendship. 说『一起工作增进友谊』，箭头是工作→友\n谊；而录音 the benefits of friendships … into their work 说的是友谊→工作。方向正好\n掉头，是预读时就提防的反向陷阱。\n借用原文：the benefits of friendships extend beyond people's social life and into their work\nC) 未提及\nC:Few people have devoted friends. 断言『很少有人有忠实朋友』，录音通篇在讲友谊的\n益处，从没下过这个数量上的极端论断，属凭空捏造。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What did researchers from an American university analyze in their initial study?", "options": ["A) The impact of friends on people's self-esteem.", "B) How supportive friends can be in the workplace.", "C) How to boost one's sense of value and worthiness.", "D) The role of family ties in people's mental well-being."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） The impact of friends on people's self-esteem.\n【定位】In an initial study of more than 700 respondents, scholars from an American university\nanalyzed the impact that friends, as opposed to family, have on self-esteem and well-being.\n在一项有 700 多名受访者的初步研究中，一所美国大学的学者分析了朋友（相对于家人）对\n自尊与幸福感的影响。\n【信号】initial study + analyzed，题干用词逐字对应。录音 In an initial study … analyzed the\nimpact that friends … have on self-esteem 把题干的 initial study、analyze 原样念出，\n这句就是首个研究的定义句；analyzed 后面跟的宾语即题干所问『分析了什么』，是直接\n的词面对应信号。\n【替换】the impact that friends, as opposed to family, have on self-esteem and well-being ↔\nThe impact of friends on people's self-esteem.\n原话与选项几乎同构，仅剪掉 well-being\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nB:How supportive friends can be in the workplace. 里的 supportive、workplace 是录\n音别处出现过的词（如 supportive friendships），但首个研究分析的是 friends 对 self-\nesteem 的影响，不是『朋友在职场多支持』，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：supportive friendships\nC) 原词陷阱\nC:How to boost one's sense of value and worthiness. 用了录音 sentiments of value\nand worthiness boost our self-esteem 里的 value、worthiness，但那是研究推出的结\n论，不是研究『分析了什么』；且 C 把它说成『如何提升』，偷换成了方法问题，答非所\n问。\n借用原文：Such sentiments of value and worthiness boost our self-esteem\nD) 张冠李戴\nD:The role of family ties in people's mental well-being. 把 family 当成研究核心，但录\n音 friends, as opposed to family 里 family 只是用来反衬朋友的对照物，研究真正聚焦的\n是 friends，选 D 等于把配角当主角。\n借用原文：friends, as opposed to family", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What did the second study find about non-work friends?", "options": ["A) They show little interest in their friends' work.", "B) They tend to be much more difficult to make.", "C) They are more trustworthy and reliable.", "D) They increase people's job satisfaction."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They increase people's job satisfaction.\n【定位】This follow-up study found that non-work friends even improve people's job satisfaction.\n这项后续研究发现，非工作场合的朋友甚至能提升人们的工作满意度。\n【信号】found that non-work friends… 直陈发现，即答案句。录音 This follow-up study\nfound that non-work friends even improve people's job satisfaction 用 found that 直\n接引出发现，主语正是题干的 non-work friends，even 还加重了语气，是把发现直接说出\n来的强信号句，听到即锁定。\n【替换】non-work friends even improve people's job satisfaction ↔\nThey increase people's job satisfaction.\nimprove = increase，job satisfaction 原样保留\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nA:They show little interest in their friends' work. 说非工作朋友几乎不关心朋友的工作，\n可录音 our preferred outlet for venting about work-related matters 恰说他们是吐槽工\n作的首选对象、很关心工作话题，意思正好相反。\n借用原文：These types of friends tend to be our preferred outlet for venting about work-\nrelated matters\nB) 未提及\nB:They tend to be much more difficult to make. 称这类朋友更难结交，录音从头到尾没\n谈过交友难易，纯属添加。\nC) 未提及\nC:They are more trustworthy and reliable. 听上去顺着全文夸朋友，但录音对 non-work\nfriends 的发现是提升工作满意度，从没说他们『更值得信赖、更可靠』，more 这个比较\n也无出处。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What does the speaker suggest managers do?", "options": ["A) Allow employees to have a flexible work schedule.", "B) Encourage employees to be friends with colleagues.", "C) Help employees balance work and family responsibilities.", "D) Organize activities to nourish friendships outside of work."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Allow employees to have a flexible work schedule.\n【定位】It's for this reason that managers should never discriminate when it comes to requests from\nemployees for flexible work arrangements.\n正因如此，管理者在面对员工提出的弹性工作安排请求时，绝不应区别对待。\n【信号】managers should never… 直接的建议句。录音 managers should never discriminate\nwhen it comes to requests from employees for flexible work arrangements 用\nmanagers should never 直陈对管理者的建议，并落到 flexible work arrangements，正\n是题干 suggest managers do 所要的那句，建议句信号明确。\n【替换】managers should never discriminate when it comes to requests from employees for flexible work\narrangements\n↔ Allow employees to have a flexible work schedule.\n『绝不区别对待弹性请求』正面化为『允许弹性安排』\n【排除】B) 答非所问\nB:Encourage employees to be friends with colleagues. 把建议窄化成『鼓励和同事交朋\n友』，但录音 non-work friends even improve people's job satisfaction 一直强调非工\n作朋友的价值，讲者给管理者的建议是弹性安排，从没让管理者撮合同事交友，答非所问。\n借用原文：non-work friends even improve people's job satisfaction\nC) 原词陷阱\nC:Help employees balance work and family responsibilities. 搬用录音 how to balance\nwork and family demands 的原词，但那是讲者谈 work-life balance 时的铺垫；他给管\n理者的落地建议是别歧视弹性安排请求，C 把铺垫当成了建议本身。\n借用原文：how to balance work and family demands\nD) 原词陷阱\nD:Organize activities to nourish friendships outside of work. 把录音 the opportunity\nto engage in a nourishing activity outside of work 的 nourishing、outside of work 原\n词搬来，听上去最耳熟，但录音说的是『员工有机会参与工作外的滋养性活动』，并没让管\n理者去『组织活动』；管理者要做的是别歧视弹性请求，张冠李戴又偷换了动作。\n借用原文：What matters is the opportunity to engage in a nourishing activity outside of work", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) surpass\n动词\n【词性槽】does not 后接动词原形\n【依据】“并不超过其他聪明动物”照应主旨\n前句说 dogs are among the more intelligent carnivores(食肉动物), social hunters and\ndomestic animals，下半句用 but 转折，意思必然是“但并不在这些类别里 X 其他聪明动\n物”。surpass 表“超过、胜过”，正合“狗虽聪明但并未超越同类”的论断，与第二段 their\nintelligence is not as __(31)__ as some researchers might have believed 互相印证。\n【竞争词】D) differentiate “区分”后通常接 between/from\ndifferentiate 表“区分”，差异辨别义，与 intelligence does not differentiate other animals\n搭不上；且 D 另有去处（见 32 题“辨别人声”）。\nJ) pledge “保证”不接动物作宾\npledge 表“承诺、保证”，主语必须是人或机构，intelligence 作主语不能 pledge animals，语\n义彻底错位。\nM) prove “证明其他动物”逻辑断裂\nprove 后通常接 that 从句或形容词补足语；intelligence does not prove other animals 不构\n成完整意思，且 M 另有去处（见 29 题）。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— outperformed 词义近、形态却错。outperformed 意为\n“胜过”，词义几乎与 surpass 一致，最容易被误选进 their intelligence does not __(26)__\nother intelligent animals。一句话防坑：does not 后必跟动词原形，outperformed 是过\n去分词/过去式，词形先被淘汰。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·does not 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) previously\n副词\n【词性槽】完成时谓语后修饰动词的状语位\n【依据】与完成时呼应的“此前已研究”\nhas examined 现在完成时强调“到目前为止已有研究”，previously 表“先前、以前”，正好\n对应“此前已有大量研究考察过狗的认知”，与下半句 found little to warrant the __(28)__\nof work 形成“前人研究虽多、却未必必要”的转折逻辑。\n【竞争词】B) approximately 近似义不修饰研究行为\napproximately 表“大约”，通常修饰数字或量，如 approximately 300，不会用来修饰\nexamined 这种研究动作。\nC) completely “完全考察”语义荒谬且与下文矛盾\ncompletely 表“彻底地”，说“研究已经彻底考察了狗的认知”与下句“认为投入的工作量并不值得”\n自相矛盾；且 C 另有去处（见 34 题）。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·完成时谓语后修饰动词的状语位"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O) volume\n名词\n【词性槽】the 与 of 短语之间的名词中心词\n【依据】“工作量之多”才能被 warrant 衡量\nwarrant 意为“证明…是必要/正当的”，宾语应是某种可被衡量的“量”。volume 表“数量、规\n模”，the volume of work 即“工作量”，承接前文 Though a significant body of research\nhas examined dog cognition __(27)__ 中的 a significant body of research，呼应“研究\n量之大是否值得”的主线，是全文论点的关键词。\n【竞争词】F) domain “工作领域”与 warrant 搭不上\ndomain 表“领域”，the domain of work 听上去是“工作的领域”，但 warrant the domain of\nwork（证明这一工作领域的合理性）逻辑别扭，且与下文“量”的比较主题不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·the 与 of 短语之间的名词中心词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) prove\n动词\n【词性槽】不定式 to 后接动词原形\n【依据】“证明狗有多聪明”而非客观研究\n本句关键是对比 to __(29)__ how clever dogs are, rather than simply to study dogs'\nbrains——前后是“证明 vs 研究”的对立。prove 后接 how 从句意为“证明狗有多聪明”，恰\n好讽刺这些研究的预设立场，与后文“狗其实没那么 formidable”的反转呼应。\n【竞争词】D) differentiate “区分”不带 how 从句\ndifferentiate 后跟 between/from + 名词，不接 how clever dogs are 这类问句式从句。\nJ) pledge “承诺狗有多聪明”不合事理\npledge 是“承诺/誓言”，研究者不会“承诺狗多聪明”，逻辑荒谬。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·不定式 to 后接动词原形"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) affirmed\n动词\n【词性槽】主语后缺谓语\n【依据】“比较结果证实了…”最贴合\naffirmed 表“证实、肯定”，后接 that 从句意为“这些比较证实了…”，与上句 Lea and a\ncolleague examined more than 300 studies of dog cognition 形成“做完比较→得出结\n论”的因果衔接，承接自然。\n【竞争词】H) outperformed “胜过”宾语应是对手而非从句\noutperformed 表“胜过”，宾语应为竞争对象（人或物），不接 that 从句；comparisons\noutperformed that... 不成句。\nI) overstated “夸大”后接概念名词，不接“狗聪明”这种事实从句\noverstated 表“夸大”，宾语多为 risk、importance、case 等抽象名词，主语 these\ncomparisons 也不会“夸大”自身得出的事实；且 I 另有去处（见 35 题）。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语后缺谓语"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) formidable\n形容词\n【词性槽】系动词 as 后的形容词作表语\n【依据】“没有大家想的那么了不起”\n整段在质疑“狗的智力被高估”：dogs are intelligent, but their intelligence is not as\n__(31)__ as some researchers might have believed。formidable 表“令人敬畏的、了不\n起的”，与 intelligent 同向但程度更强，正好对应“没研究者以为得那么强”的反差。\n【竞争词】L) prospective “潜在的智力”与同级比较语义错位\nprospective 表“未来的、有望的”，多修饰人或角色（prospective student/buyer），不修饰\nintelligence；且与 as...as 比较“以前有多相信”逻辑不通。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— distinct 在 33 题用过、勿用过即划失败。distinct 也是形\n容词、且“独特的智力”乍听通顺，主体考生若没坚持“用过即划”，容易在 31 题二次选 E，导\n致 33 题反而崩盘。防坑：每填一空立刻在草稿上划掉该字母。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·系动词 as 后的形容词作表语"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) differentiate\n动词\n【词性槽】情态动词 + 并列动词原形\n【依据】与 recognize 并列形成“识别+辨别”\nrecognize 是“认出”，differentiate 是“辨别（区分不同的）”，二者构成“识别并区分人声”的\n常见认知能力描述，且呼应下句 remember a greater number of __(33)__ human voices\n中 distinct 的“不同”主题——能 differentiate 才谈得上记住多少 distinct voices。\n【竞争词】J) pledge “狗保证人声”完全不通\npledge 表“承诺、立誓”，主语是猫狗、宾语是人声，无法构成任何合理含义。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·情态动词 + 并列动词原形"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) distinct\n形容词\n【词性槽】名词前作定语\n【依据】“不同的人声”才好谈“记住多少种”\n本句讨论 which species can remember a greater number of __(33)__ human voices，\n要比较谁能记住更多“不同的”人声。distinct 意为“截然不同的、可分辨的”，与 a greater\nnumber of 搭配自然，正好突出“记住的人声种类多寡”这一比较点。\n【竞争词】G) formidable “强大的人声”不通\nformidable 多形容“令人生畏的对手/任务”，搭配 voices 在语义和搭配上都怪异；且 G 另有去\n处（见 31 题）。\nL) prospective “潜在的人声”逻辑跑偏\nprospective 表“未来的、潜在的”，多修饰 buyer、employer、student 这类“未来角色”，与“记\n住已经听过的人声数量”这个语境不搭。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词前作定语"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) completely\n副词\n【词性槽】助动词 do 与实义动词 agree 之间的状语\n【依据】“完全同意”与下文反对意见呼应\n原文紧接出现 Zachary Silver, an American researcher, believes the authors of the new\nstudy __(35)__ the idea，表明确有研究者持不同看法。因此“并非所有研究者都\ncompletely（完全）赞同这一结论”最贴切——not...completely 是地道的部分否定表达。\n【竞争词】B) approximately “大约同意”不像人话\napproximately 表数量上的近似，不与 agree 搭配；agree approximately with 不是英语\n习语。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·助动词 do 与实义动词 agree 之"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) affirmed", "B) approximately", "C) completely", "D) differentiate", "E) distinct", "F) domain", "G) formidable", "H) outperformed", "I) overstated", "J) pledge", "K) previously", "L) prospective", "M) prove", "N) surpass", "O) volume"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) overstated\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 + 谓语 + 宾语的过去式动词\n【依据】“夸大了‘狗研究过多’的论断”才符合反对者立场\nZachary Silver 持反对意见，认为新研究 the authors of the new study __(35)__ the idea\nthat an excessive amount of research has been devoted to dogs, as the field of dog\ncognition is young, and there is much to be learned——下半句给出原因（领域年轻、\n还有很多要学），正是为了说明新研究“言过其实”。overstated 表“夸大”，与 overstated\nthe idea/case 是固定搭配，精准命中。\n【竞争词】H) outperformed “胜过观点”搭配荒谬\noutperformed 表“在表现上胜过”，宾语应是竞争者；overperform the idea 不构成英语表达，\n与反对立场也无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 + 谓语 + 宾语的过去式动词"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "It does us far more good to focus on things we can be grateful for than what makes us sad and resentful.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】对比聚焦点的核心命题\n【定位】Simply put, for most of us, it is far more helpful to focus on the things in life for which we\ncan express gratitude than those that incline us toward resentment and lamentation.\n简单地说，对我们大多数人而言，专注于生活中那些值得感恩之事，远比专注于让人心生怨\n恨、悲叹之事更有益处。\n【改写】does us far more good ↔ far more helpful\n比较级同义替换\nfocus on things we can be grateful for ↔\nfocus on the things in life for which we can express gratitude\n动词到名词的同义改写\nwhat makes us sad and resentful ↔ those that incline us toward resentment and lamentation\n形容词与抽象名词的同义对换\n【辨邻】 D When we focus on the things we regret… we tend to become more regretful 与\nwhen we focus on the things we are grateful for, a greater sense of happiness\ntends to spread through our lives 只是分别从两端各说一面，没有把“X 远比 Y 更有\n益”这种比较关系整合在一句里；陈述句的 does us far more good 这一比较关系，只\n有 C 段的 far more helpful 直接承载。\nWhen we focus on the things we regret, such as failed relationships, family disputes, and setbacks\nin career and finance, we tend to become more regretful. Conversely, when we focus on the things\nwe are grateful for, a greater sense of happiness tends to spread through our lives.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The beneficial impacts of gratitude can extend from individuals to their community and to the wider society.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】影响范围从个体扩散到社会\n【定位】Broadly speaking, a community in which people feel grateful to one another is likely to be a\nmore pleasant place to live than one characterized by mutual suspicion and resentment. The\nbeneficial effects of gratitude may extend even further.\n总体来说，一个彼此感恩的社区，比一个充满相互猜忌与怨恨的社区更适合居住。感恩的有\n益影响甚至可能延伸得更远。\n【改写】individuals ↔ grateful people may be more likely to reciprocate\n从抽象到具体的同义对应\ncommunity ↔ a community in which people feel grateful to one another\n关键词原样复现\nthe wider society ↔ The beneficial effects of gratitude may extend even further\n范围扩展的同义表达\nbeneficial impacts ↔ The beneficial effects of gratitude\n近义词替换\n【辨邻】 H Gratitude also tends to strengthen a sense of connection with others 与 they\nare more likely to choose to spend their time with one another 都停留在双人或\n小圈子的连接，没有出现 community/wider society 这样的范围扩张词，无法覆盖陈\n述句的“扩散至社区与更广社会”。\nGratitude also tends to strengthen a sense of connection with others. When people want to do good\nthings that inspire gratitude, the level of dedication in relationships tends to grow and relationships\nseem to last longer.\nM those who adopt it as a habit are enriching not only their own lives but also the\nlives of those around them 只把益处扩到 those around them（周围他人），没有\n出现 community 或 wider society 的明确扩展。\nthose who adopt it as a habit are enriching not only their own lives but also the lives of those\naround them", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "The participants in a recent study repeatedly underestimated the positive effect on those who received thank-you notes.", "options": null, "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一项新研究 + 反复低估收信人正面反应\n【定位】One recent study asked participants to write a note of thanks to someone and then\nestimate how surprised and happy the recipient would feel—an impact that they\nconsistently underestimated.\n最近一项研究让参与者给某人写一封感谢信，然后预估收信人会有多么惊讶与开心——而他\n们对这一影响始终估计得过低。\n【改写】The participants in a recent study ↔ One recent study asked participants\n句式重构与关键词原样复现\nthose who received thank-you notes ↔ write a note of thanks to someone\n角色视角互换\nrepeatedly underestimated the positive effect ↔ an impact that they consistently underestimated\n近义副词与同根名词替换\n【辨邻】 F Individuals who regularly engage in gratitude exercises, such as counting their\nblessings or expressing gratitude to others 描述的是常规感恩练习与人际关系/生\n理症状的关系，没有“写感谢信 + 预估收信人反应 + 持续低估”这一组实验细节。\nRecent scientific studies support these ancient teachings. Individuals who regularly engage in\ngratitude exercises, such as counting their blessings or expressing gratitude to others, exhibit\nincreased satisfaction with relationships and fewer symptoms of physical illness.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Good deeds can sometimes make people feel uncomfortable.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】好事也会让人不舒服\n【定位】Of course, acts of kindness can also foster discomfort.\n当然，善举有时也会让人感到不适。\n【改写】Good deeds ↔ acts of kindness\n同义替换\nmake people feel uncomfortable ↔ foster discomfort\n形容词与同根名词的同义改写\nsometimes ↔ can also\n情态副词的同义对应", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "People who regularly express gratitude can benefit in moral terms.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】感恩带来道德层面的益处\n【定位】And the benefits are not only psychological and physical. They may also be moral—those\nwho practice gratitude also view their lives less materialistically and suffer from less envy.\n而且这些益处不仅是心理和生理上的，还可能是道德上的——践行感恩的人对生活的物质化\n看法更少、嫉妒心也更少。\n【改写】People who regularly express gratitude ↔ those who practice gratitude\n近义动词与隐含的常态意思\nbenefit in moral terms ↔ They may also be moral\n名词加修饰词与“系动词 + 形容词”的同义\n【辨邻】 G expressing gratitude encourages others to continue being generous, thus\npromoting a virtuous cycle of goodness in relationships 谈的是关系层面的善意循\n环，Some are inspired to attempt to become better people themselves 偏“成为\n更好的人”，整体没有出现 moral 这个明确的道德维度词，与陈述句要求的 in moral\nterms 错位。\nexpressing gratitude encourages others to continue being generous, thus promoting a virtuous cycle\nof goodness in relationships", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "A basketball coach advocated performing generous acts without expecting anything in return.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】篮球教练 + 不求回报\n【定位】In saying this, Wooden sought to promote purely generous acts, as opposed to those\nperformed with an expectation of reward.\n伍登说这番话，意在倡导纯粹的慷慨之举，而不是那些抱着期待回报心态去做的善行。\n【改写】A basketball coach ↔ the great basketball coach John Wooden\n上位词到具体专名的同义对应\nperforming generous acts ↔ purely generous acts\n动词化原文的名词短语\nwithout expecting anything in return ↔\nas opposed to those performed with an expectation of reward\n把对照式表达压缩为否定修饰\n【辨邻】 G expressing gratitude encourages others to continue being generous 与 grateful\npeople may be more likely to reciprocate 谈的是慷慨与回报的循环，但通篇没有提\n及任何篮球教练，与陈述句明确要求的 a basketball coach 错位。\nexpressing gratitude encourages others to continue being generous, thus promoting a virtuous cycle\nof goodness in relationships. Similarly, grateful people may be more likely to reciprocate with acts\nof kindness of their own.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "More and more evidence shows it makes us mentally and physically healthier to routinely count our blessings.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】越来越多证据 + 心身健康\n【定位】And while no one would argue for cultivating a false sense of blessedness, there is mounting\nevidence that counting our blessings is one of the best habits we can develop to promote\nmental and physical health.\n尽管没人会主张刻意培养一种虚假的幸福感，但越来越多的证据表明，时常细数自己的福分\n是我们能养成的最佳习惯之一，对身心健康都有益。\n【改写】More and more evidence ↔ mounting evidence\n近义形容词替换\nroutinely count our blessings ↔ counting our blessings is one of the best habits we can develop\n副词与“习惯”概念的等值\nmentally and physically healthier ↔ promote mental and physical health\n形容词比较级与“促进健康”的同义\n【辨邻】 F Recent scientific studies support these ancient teachings 只用 recent scientific\nstudies 而非 mounting evidence；increased satisfaction with relationships and\nfewer symptoms of physical illness 侧重关系满意度与生理症状，没有同时出现“心\n理 + 生理健康”的双指纹。\nRecent scientific studies support these ancient teachings. Individuals who regularly engage in\ngratitude exercises, such as counting their blessings or expressing gratitude to others, exhibit\nincreased satisfaction with relationships and fewer symptoms of physical illness.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "Of all states of mind, feeling grateful is considered one of the most healthy and beneficial.", "options": null, "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】感恩是最健康、最有益的心态之一\n【定位】Gratitude is one of the healthiest and most nourishing of all states of mind, and those\nwho adopt it as a habit are enriching not only their own lives but also the lives of those\naround them.\n感恩是所有心态中最健康、最滋养的心态之一；养成感恩习惯的人，不仅在丰盈自己的生\n活，也在丰盈身边人的生活。\n【改写】Of all states of mind ↔ of all states of mind\n短语原样复现\nfeeling grateful ↔ Gratitude\n动名词与抽象名词同义\none of the most healthy and beneficial ↔ one of the healthiest and most nourishing\n最高级框架内的近义形容词替换\n【辨邻】 D counting our blessings is one of the best habits we can develop to promote\nmental and physical health 中的 one of the best 修饰的是 habits，而陈述句要求\nof all states of mind 中的最高级修饰对象是“心态”，对象错位即不构成匹配。\ncounting our blessings is one of the best habits we can develop to promote mental and\nphysical health", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The principles underlying the research into gratitude are nothing new at all.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】感恩研究背后的原理并不新\n【定位】While this research into gratitude is relatively new, the principles involved are anything but.\n虽然这类关于感恩的研究相对较新，但其中所涉的原理一点都不新。\n【改写】The principles underlying the research into gratitude ↔\nthis research into gratitude is relatively new, the principles involved\n句式重构与同义替换\nare nothing new at all ↔ the principles involved are anything but\n习语级强否定的同义\n【辨邻】 E Gratitude has long enjoyed a privileged position in many of the world's cultural\ntraditions 与 some ancient Western philosophers counsel gratitude 谈的是古\n代文化态度，没有出现“研究背后的原理一点都不新”这一对照句框架，与陈述句要求\n错位。\nGratitude has long enjoyed a privileged position in many of the world's cultural traditions.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "Gratitude is likely to enhance one's sense of being connected with other people.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】增强与他人的连接感\n【定位】Gratitude also tends to strengthen a sense of connection with others.\n感恩也倾向于增强人与他人之间的连接感。\n【改写】is likely to enhance ↔ tends to strengthen\n近义动词与情态副词同义\nsense of being connected with other people ↔ sense of connection with others\n动词过去分词与同根名词同义\n【辨邻】 G a virtuous cycle of goodness in relationships 谈的是 relationships 中的善意循\n环，并未直接讲“增强连接感”；陈述句的“sense of being connected”需对 H 段的\nsense of connection 主题句，与 G 段错位。\nexpressing gratitude encourages others to continue being generous, thus promoting a virtuous cycle\nof goodness in relationships.", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about the deeper implications of AI?", "options": ["A) It is causing catastrophic levels of unemployment.", "B) It is doing physical harm to human operators.", "C) It is altering moral judgments.", "D) It is reshaping humanity."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干What do we learn about the deeper implications of AI?是直接问对某概念了解到的\n内容，没有infer/imply等推断信号。\n【拆句】Q46是一个标准的what引导的特殊疑问句，主干是\"What do we learn about X\"，about\n后接the deeper implications of AI整个名词短语，无陷阱。\n【定位】While the term AI conjures up anxieties about killer robots or catastrophic levels of\nunemployment, there are other, deeper implications. As AI increasingly shapes the human\nexperience, how does this change what it means to be human?\n虽然\"AI\"一词让人联想到杀人机器人或灾难性的失业，但还有其他更深层的影响。随着AI日\n益塑造人类经验，这将如何改变\"作为人\"的含义？\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\n原文恰恰把catastrophic levels of unemployment列为人们对AI的浅层焦虑，作者要说\n的\"更深层影响\"是与之对立的——A把作者明示否定的浅层焦虑拿来当深层影响，方向\n反了。\nB) 无中生有\n全文从未提及AI对操作者造成身体伤害，physical harm属凭空虚构。\nC) 以偏概全\n道德判断（moral implications）只是judgments that have moral implications里举\n的一个具体例子，是\"改变人之为人\"的下属支撑，不是\"deeper implications\"本身。\nD) ✓ 正确\nTechnological innovations reshape people as they use these innovations ↔\nIt is reshaping humanity.\nhow does this change what it means to be human ↔ It is reshaping humanity.\n预判\"改变人本身\"，四个选项里只有It is reshaping humanity.在说人本身被改写，正面\n命中。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What is the consequence of algorithmic prescription replacing human judgment?", "options": ["A) People lose the chance to cultivate the ability to make practical judgments.", "B) People are prevented from participating in making major decisions in the workplace.", "C) Managers no longer have the chance to decide which loan to approve.", "D) Managers do not need to take the trouble to determine who to hire or fire."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What is the consequence of …是问某行为带来的具体后果，答案在原文有明确对应句而\n非需推断。\n【拆句】Q47结构清晰：主干what is the consequence of X，of后接动名词短语algorithmic\nprescription replacing human judgment，其中replacing为现在分词作定语修饰\nprescription，相当于\"算法处方替代了人的判断\"。\n【定位】These are areas where algorithmic prescription is replacing human judgment, and so people\nwho might have had the chance to develop practical judgment in these areas no longer will.\n这些就是算法处方正在取代人类判断的领域，因此本来可能有机会在这些领域培养实践判断\n力的人，不再有此机会。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\npeople who might have had the chance to develop practical judgment in these areas no longer\nwill\n↔ People lose the chance to cultivate the ability to make practical judgments.\n预判正是\"失去培养判断力的机会\"，People lose the chance to cultivate the ability\nto make practical judgments.逐字对应。\nB) 偷换概念\n原文是\"失去培养判断力的练习机会\"，B把它偷换成\"被阻止参与重大决策\"——重大决策\n的参与权并未被剥夺，被剥夺的是\"练手\"机会。\nC) 张冠李戴\n原文说被剥夺机会的是普通人（people），不是经理（managers）；经理本是行使判\n断的一方，正被算法替代。\nD) 与原文相反\n原文是中性陈述\"经理们日常会做这些决定\"，并未说他们觉得麻烦或希望被替代；D把负\n面后果\"失去机会\"扭曲成正面\"省事\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "What may result from increasing application of recommendation engines in our consumption of culture?", "options": ["A) Consumers will have much limited choice.", "B) Consumers will actually enjoy better luck.", "C) It will be easier to decide on what to enjoy.", "D) Humans will develop tastes similar to machines'."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What may result from …直接问由某事导致的具体结果，是典型的细节题问法。\n【拆句】Q48主干what may result from X，may弱化了语气表\"可能性\"，X是动名词短语\nincreasing application of recommendation engines in our consumption of culture，\n其中increasing修饰application，整体意为\"推荐引擎在文化消费里日益增多的应用\"。\n【定位】Recommendation engines, which are increasingly prevalent intermediaries in people's\nconsumption of culture, may serve to constrain choice and minimize luck.\n推荐引擎在人们的文化消费中日益普遍地充当中介，可能会限制选择、削减偶然。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nmay serve to constrain choice and minimize luck ↔\nConsumers will have much limited choice.\n预判\"限制选择\"，Consumers will have much limited choice.恰是其形容词化版本，\n正面对应。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文是minimize luck（削减偶然/运气），B却说\"享受更好的运气\"——意思完全反向。\nC) 以偏概全\n原文中\"机器能扫描更广范围\"只是让步性铺垫（\"In one sense, this is helpful\"），紧接\nP6\"At the same time, though\"明确转折，作者的核心结论是限制选择，而不是\"决定更\n容易\"。\nD) 偷换概念\n原文是\"用机器口味替代人的口味\"（machine taste replaces human taste），即人的\n口味被压制；D却说\"人将培养出与机器相似的口味\"，主客体反了。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is likely to happen to larger parts of our daily life as machine learning algorithms improve?", "options": ["A) They will turn out to be more pleasant.", "B) They will repeat our past experience.", "C) They can be completely anticipated.", "D) They may become better and better."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What is likely to happen to … as …是问条件下将发生的具体情况，找原文对应预测句\n即可。\n【拆句】Q49结构是\"What is likely to happen to A as B\"，as引导时间/条件状语从句，意为\"随着\nB，A身上可能发生什么\"；考生易把as误读为\"因为\"，这里其实更接近\"随着\"。\n【定位】As machine learning algorithms improve and as they train on more extensive data sets, larger\nparts of everyday life are likely to become utterly predictable.\n随着机器学习算法的改进、以及在更大数据集上的训练，日常生活越来越大的部分将变得完\n全可被预测。\n【选项】A) 答非所问\nP7主句的核心结论是utterly predictable；more efficient and pleasant是其后的次\n级描述。题干问的是larger parts of daily life本身会如何，而非体验如何——A答非\n所问。\nB) 偷换概念\nconstrained by their past说的是推荐引擎按过去偏好限制选择，与P7\"算法越准、生活\n越可预测\"是两段不同论点；B把前一段的素材搬来回答后一段的问题，错位。\nC) ✓ 正确\nlarger parts of everyday life are likely to become utterly predictable ↔\nThey can be completely anticipated.\n预判核心是\"完全可预测\"，They can be completely anticipated.中\ncompletely+anticipated双重对应，是唯一保留\"utterly\"程度的选项。\nD) 张冠李戴\n原文是\"预测会越来越好\"（The predictions are going to get better and better），\n即\"算法预测\"这个东西本身越来越好；D却把better and better的主语挂在larger parts\nof daily life上，主语错位。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "Why does the author say the creatures living in the more and more AI-mediated world will become increasingly unlike us?", "options": ["A) They will have lost the most significant human element of being intelligent.", "B) They will no longer possess the human characteristic of being unpredictable.", "C) They will not be able to understand themselves as we can do today.", "D) They will be deprived of what their predecessors were proud of about themselves."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干Why does the author say …问的是作者作出某判断的直接依据，原文末段有显性原因\n句，不需推断。\n【拆句】Q50是Why疑问句，宾语从句中the creatures …是主语，living in …是分词后置定语，will\nbecome是主句谓语；表面长，骨架是\"creatures will become unlike us\"。\n【定位】But to the extent that unpredictability is part of how people understand themselves and part of\nwhat people like about themselves, humanity is in the process of losing something significant.\nAs they become more and more predictable, the creatures inhabiting the increasingly AI-\nmediated world will become less and less like us.\n但是，由于不可预测性本身就是人类自我认知与自我喜爱的一部分，人类正在失去某种重要\n的东西。随着他们变得越来越可预测，居住在愈发AI化的世界里的这些\"生物\"，将越来越不\n像我们。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n全文从未把intelligent列为人类最重要特征；作者强调的是unpredictability。A自行虚\n构了一种\"智能\"的核心人性。\nB) ✓ 正确\nunpredictability is part of how people understand themselves and part of what people like about\nthemselves\n↔ They will no longer possess the human characteristic of being unpredictable.\nAs they become more and more predictable ↔\nThey will no longer possess the human characteristic of being unpredictable.\n预判核心词是unpredictability，四个选项中只有They will no longer possess the\nhuman characteristic of being unpredictable.点出unpredictable，正面命中。\nC) 过度推断\n原文说unpredictability是\"how people understand themselves\"的一部分，并没有\n说\"未来人将无法理解自己\"——C把作为成分的unpredictability当成了理解能力本身。\nD) 以偏概全\nD只说\"被剥夺了前人曾为之自豪的东西\"——但\"前人曾为之自豪的东西\"具体是什么，D\n避而不答；正确答案B则点明那就是unpredictability。Why题要的是具体原因，不是含\n糊代称。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about phonics in many American classrooms?", "options": ["A) It is ill reputed.", "B) It is mostly misapplied.", "C) It is arbitrarily excluded.", "D) It is misrepresented."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn about phonics in many American classrooms? 用 What do we\nlearn about 直接索取关于某物的客观信息，对应原文应有一句明确表态。\n【拆句】结构平铺直叙：What do we learn about phonics in many American classrooms? 是一\n个简单的特殊疑问句，about phonics 是 learn 的宾语补足部分，in many American\nclassrooms 是地点状语，无陷阱。\n【定位】But in many classrooms, this can be a dirty word. So much so that some teachers have had to\nsneak phonics teaching materials into the classroom.\n但在许多课堂里，它（拼读法）几乎成了一个令人嫌弃的词。以至于一些老师不得不偷偷把\n拼读法教材带进教室。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthis can be a dirty word ↔ It is ill reputed.\nsome teachers have had to sneak phonics teaching materials into the classroom ↔ ill reputed\n预判得到 phonics 在课堂里名声不好；四个选项里只有 It is ill reputed. 把 dirty word\n的核心语义 \"被嫌弃/名声差\" 直接压缩出来，与预判同义。\nB) 无中生有\nIt is mostly misapplied. 指 phonics 被错误地使用，但定位句只说它名声差、被偷偷带\n进教室，并未说它被 \"误用\"。\nC) 过度推断\nIt is arbitrarily excluded. 指被武断地排除在课堂之外，但 Most teachers today,\nalmost three out of four ... use a mix called \"balanced literacy\" 表明 phonics 至少\n被以混合形式部分使用，并未被武断排除；且 Q51 问的是 in many classrooms 的状\n况，不是普遍排除。\nD) 无中生有\nIt is misrepresented. 指被歪曲/错误地呈现，但定位句谈论的是它在课堂氛围中受冷\n遇，并不涉及 \"歪曲\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What has America been witnessing for decades?", "options": ["A) An obsession with innovating teaching methodologies of reading.", "B) An enduring debate over the approach to teaching children to read.", "C) An increasing concern with many children's inadequacy in literacy.", "D) An ever-forceful advocacy of a combined method for teaching reading."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What has America been witnessing for decades? 索取一个客观的、贯穿数十年的事\n实，应在原文找到对应陈述。\n【拆句】What has America been witnessing for decades? 主干 America has been witnessing\nwhat，for decades 是持续性时间状语，与现在完成进行时呼应。\n【定位】America is stuck in a debate about teaching children to read that has been going on\nfor decades.\n美国陷入了一场关于如何教孩子阅读的争论，这场争论已经持续了几十年。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nAn obsession with innovating teaching methodologies of reading. 把核心从 \"争\n论\" 偷换成 \"对创新阅读教学法的痴迷\"，原文谈的是两派持续对立而非痴迷创新。\nB) ✓ 正确\na debate about teaching children to read ↔\nAn enduring debate over the approach to teaching children to read.\nhas been going on for decades ↔ enduring\nB 项把定位句里 \"持续数十年\" + \"关于教孩子阅读的争论\" 两个核心元素分别压缩为\nenduring + debate over the approach to teaching children to read，与预判精准\n对应。\nC) 偷换概念\nAn increasing concern with many children's inadequacy in literacy. 把对象换成 \"对\n许多孩子识字不足的担忧\"，原文 P2 提到识字率低只是后果数据，并不是 \"美国数十年\n来一直在见证\" 的主体。\nD) 与原文相反\nAn ever-forceful advocacy of a combined method for teaching reading. 说 \"对组\n合法越来越有力的倡导\"，但原文明确通过 Tenette Smith 说 This combination of\nmethods is ineffective，与选项方向相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Why does Tenette Smith think a combination of teaching methods is ineffective?", "options": ["A) Elementary schoolchildren will be frustrated when taught with several methods combined.", "B) Phonics has to be systematically applied and clearly taught to achieve the desired effect.", "C) Sprinkling in a little phonics deters the progress of even adequately motivated children.", "D) Balanced literacy fails to sustain children's interest in developing a good reading habit."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】Why does Tenette Smith think a combination of teaching methods is ineffective? 用\nWhy 索取原因，对应原文里这位人物所说的解释。\n【拆句】Why does Tenette Smith think a combination of teaching methods is ineffective?\nWhy 引导，think 后接 that 从句（that 省略），从句主语是 a combination of teaching\nmethods，谓语 is ineffective，结构清晰。\n【定位】\"You can't sprinkle in a little phonics,\" says Tenette Smith, executive director of elementary\neducation and reading at Mississippi's education department. \"It has to be systematic and\nexplicitly taught.\"\n\"你不能只是零星地撒点拼读法进去\"，密西西比州教育部基础教育与阅读执行主任 Tenette\nSmith 说，\"它必须被系统、明确地教授。\"\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nElementary schoolchildren will be frustrated when taught with several methods\ncombined. 把 ineffective 的原因换成 \"孩子受挫\"，原文未提及任何 frustration。\nB) ✓ 正确\nIt has to be systematic and explicitly taught. ↔\nPhonics has to be systematically applied and clearly taught to achieve the desired effect.\nYou can't sprinkle in a little phonics ↔ Phonics has to be systematically applied\n选项 Phonics has to be systematically applied and clearly taught to achieve the\ndesired effect. 把 Tenette Smith 引号里的两条要求 systematic + explicitly taught\n完整保留，与预判一致。\nC) 过度推断\nSprinkling in a little phonics deters the progress of even adequately motivated\nchildren. 把原文的 \"不能零星撒拼读法\" 错误延伸为 \"零星撒拼读法会阻碍连足够有动力\n的孩子的进步\"，文中并未谈到 \"motivated children\" 或被 \"deter\" 这一具体后果。\nD) 偷换概念\nBalanced literacy fails to sustain children's interest in developing a good reading\nhabit. 把无效的原因换成 \"无法维持孩子对培养良好阅读习惯的兴趣\"，原文 Tenette\nSmith 完全没谈兴趣/习惯。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What does the author say Mississippi's success is attributed to?", "options": ["A) Convening a National Reading Panel to synthesize research evidence.", "B) Placing sufficient emphasis upon both fluency and comprehension.", "C) Adopting scientifically grounded approaches to teaching reading.", "D) Obtaining support from Congress to upgrade teaching methods."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say Mississippi's success is attributed to? 直接索取一个归因事\n实，是细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author say Mississippi's success is attributed to? 嵌套结构：What 是\nattributed to 的宾语前置；the author say 是插入的引述层；核心是 Mississippi's\nsuccess is attributed to what。\n【定位】Mississippi's success is attributed to implementing reading methods supported by a body of\nresearch known as the science of reading.\n密西西比州的成功要归因于实施了由一整套被称为 \"阅读科学\" 的研究所支持的阅读教\n学法。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nConvening a National Reading Panel to synthesize research evidence. 把 1997 年\n国会召集小组这一历史背景错当成密西西比成功的直接归因，但原文归因句的对象是\nimplementing reading methods，不是 convening a panel。\nB) 以偏概全\nPlacing sufficient emphasis upon both fluency and comprehension. 取的是 panel\n结论 phonics, along with explicit instruction in phonemic awareness, fluency and\ncomprehension 中的两个并列项，但归因对象是整体阅读科学，不是 fluency +\ncomprehension 这两点。\nC) ✓ 正确\nreading methods supported by a body of research known as the science of reading ↔\nAdopting scientifically grounded approaches to teaching reading.\nimplementing reading methods ↔ Adopting ... approaches to teaching reading\n选项 C 把 \"实施\" \"由研究支持的\" \"阅读教学法\" 三要素分别压缩为 Adopting、\nscientifically grounded、approaches to teaching reading，与预判精确对应。\nD) 无中生有\nObtaining support from Congress to upgrade teaching methods. 误把 1997 年国\n会的角色解读为对密西西比的支持，但原文中国会只是请专家组综述证据，并未对密西\n西比直接 upgrade 教学法。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2023-06-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What have advances in statistics and brain imaging proved ineffective?", "options": ["A) The teaching of symbol-sound relationships.", "B) Explicit instruction in phonemic awareness.", "C) Efforts to end the reading wars.", "D) The immersive approach."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What have advances in statistics and brain imaging proved ineffective? 索取一个具体\n被证伪的对象，是细节题。\n【拆句】What have advances in statistics and brain imaging proved ineffective? 主干\nadvances have proved what ineffective，What 同时充当 proved 的宾语，ineffective\n是宾语补足语；此处 prove 接复合宾语，意为 证明某物是无效的。\n【定位】But advances in statistics and brain imaging have disproved the whole-language method.\n但统计学和脑成像方面的进展已经证伪了整体语言法。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThe teaching of symbol-sound relationships. 取自 Some advocate teaching\nsymbol-sound relationships ... known as phonics，但被证伪的对象是 whole-\nlanguage，而 phonics 在文中被反复认定为 the best way to teach children\nto read。\nB) 与原文相反\nExplicit instruction in phonemic awareness. 取自 phonics, along with explicit\ninstruction in phonemic awareness, fluency and comprehension, worked best，\n是 panel 推荐有效的做法，并非被证伪的对象。\nC) 答非所问\nEfforts to end the reading wars. 来自 end the \"reading wars\"，但原文并未说这种努\n力被证伪；被证伪的是具体的 whole-language method。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe whole-language method ↔ The immersive approach.\ndisproved ↔ proved ineffective\n本题难度在于：题干用 proved ineffective、原文用 disproved、选项用 The\nimmersive approach. 而非 the whole-language method——只有读出 P3 里\nimmersive approach = whole language 的等值，才能锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
